        "title": "Google should have gone the GTA 6 route with its Pixel 11 show; instead, we got the Trevor Noah cringe fest",
        "link": "https://www.androidcentral.com/phones/google-pixel/made-by-google-2026-keynote-was-a-cringe-fest",
        "source": "Latest from Android Central",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.365047+00:00",
        "image": "https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/EZP7tPgiqWR9UnobgMvZ8G-2000-80.jpg",
        "full_content": "Google should have gone the GTA 6 route with its Pixel 11 show; instead, we got the Trevor Noah cringe fest\nWhy did they do a show eight hours AFTER the phones were announced??\nGoogle launched its brand-new Pixel 11 series and Pixel Watch 5 yesterday at 10 am, and then, eight hours later, re-announced the entire series in an awkward, 1.5-hour-long online presentation hosted by comedic sensation Trevor Noah.\nThe show was broadcast on YouTube, and at one point only 57,000 people were watching, but I think it did go up to 80,000. Comments were also turned off, and I’m not surprised why.\nThere were only two really amazing parts of the show: the first was when Joël Yawili, a Senior Product Manager for the Gemini app, appeared on the show, and the second was when the company announced a new accessibility feature called Sign-to-Text, which I’ll get to later.\nI want to first focus on the show. There were some compelling moments when I thought it could have worked, but overall, I think what Google was trying to do wasn’t executed the way the company wanted.\nTrevor Noah vs. Google\nThe whole point of a lifestyle show catered to consumers was to communicate how amazing Pixels are. And they are! In my hands-on with the Pixel 11 series, I wrote that the company has actually upped the game for AI on smartphones.\nAnd asking Trevor Noah to host could have been a smart move.\nBut the whole show was a cringe fest. And I think it’s because you’re watching someone like Noah, who is super casual, a comedian who knows how to host a show, next to someone from Google who was clearly PR trained and was given a very specific script to follow.\nGet the latest news from Android Central, your trusted companion in the world of Android\nNoah seemed bored, and at times seemed very uninterested in wanting to be there. He even tried to make a very obvious dick joke that clearly wasn’t well received by the Google product expert who was talking with him. The audience did laugh, though (and so did I).\nWhat was Google trying to do with this event?\nI think this show was a very obvious move by Google. It was a way to connect with consumers and help them understand their phones and what the new devices can do.\nTraditionally, launch shows are hosted mostly to present to developers, tech enthusiasts, bloggers, influencers, and the media. And the message is then disseminated to the rest of the world through articles, photos, interviews, etc.\nWe’ve seen these shows evolve to something more spectacular, with special guests, celebrities, and even athletes to show off how they use these phones — all an effort to better connect with consumers. Because at the end of the day, that’s the biggest challenge for any tech company: How do you connect people with your product so they will buy it?\nLast year was the first time that Google went completely out of the box, and to be fair, the company put on a pretty cool show. Jimmy Fallon hosted, and everything was pretty exciting… and it made sense because it was 10 years of the Pixel. So doing something spectacular was logical.\nAnd I would have been completely fine if Google wanted to do the same thing again this year. What really threw me for a loop was the decision to host the show… eight hours after the phones were announced and made public.\nAt that point you’ve completely lost the audience. Consumers already know about the phones; they are reading our articles, they’re looking at what influencers are posting, and they have no real reason to go to a show, even one hosted by Trevor Noah.\nWhile it makes sense to stream the event on YouTube, since Google controls the platform, a better move would have been to partner with a streaming service such as Netflix or Paramount Plus. An example of a showcase like this is Grand Theft Auto 6: An Extended Look, premiering on Netflix later this month, showing that this is clearly an avenue other companies are exploring to showcase new and upcoming launches.\nIt could even have been something short-lived, like WWE’s annual WrestleMania, which is now streamed on Netflix. And it could have been titled “Trevor Noah presents … the Google Pixel 11.”\nThe best parts of the show\nFor me, when Joël Yawili, a Senior Product Manager at Google, came on the show, there was a visible positive shift in the conversation. At first, Noah once again came across as uninterested and even said, “another demo,” almost as if he were really bored.\nBut when Yawili began to speak, he didn’t start with a script. He decided to shift the focus to a genuine connection he seemed to have with Noah: They grew up in the same town, it turns out. It also helped that Yawili seemed to genuinely care about the product he’s worked on. The entire interaction and demo truly made me smile.\nI also loved how, in Yawili’s demonstration, he brought in a text conversation he was having with his mother, and the way he did it was so casual that it made me want to keep watching.\nThe second time the show hit for me was when Google showed off its new Sign-to-Text feature. Watching how it was demonstrated was fascinating, and the two individuals on stage really seemed interested in what they were doing and how this should have a huge impact on many people in the hard-of-hearing and -speaking community.\nI’ll end this by saying I don’t think Google did a bad job; I just think it wasn’t thoroughly thought out. I think the show could have been executed a bit differently, and I think it shouldn’t have been done eight hours after the products were announced.\nGoogle was doing the right thing by wanting to connect with consumers, and I think it can — just with a few tweaks. Are there patches for a live event?\nShruti Shekar is Android Central's Editor-in-Chief. She was born in India, brought up in Singapore, but now lives in Toronto. She started her journalism career as a political reporter in Ottawa, Canada's capital, and then made her foray into tech journalism at MobileSyrup and most recently at Yahoo Finance Canada. When work isn't on her mind, she loves working out, reading, watching the Raptors, and planning what she's going to eat the next day.\nYou must confirm your public display name before commenting\nPlease logout and then login again, you will then be prompted to enter your display name.",
        "content": "Google should have gone the GTA 6 route with its Pixel 11 show; instead, we got the Trevor Noah cringe fest\nWhy did they do a show eight hours AFTER the phones were announced??\nGoogle launched its brand-new Pixel 11 series and Pixel Watch 5 yesterday at 10 am, and then, eight hours later, re-announced the entire series in an awkward, 1.5-hour-long online presentation hosted by comedic sensation Tr...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "5m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "Is it a phone or a gimbal? This is Honor's new Robot Phone!",
        "link": "https://www.androidcentral.com/phones/honor-phones/this-is-honors-new-robot-phone",
        "source": "Latest from Android Central",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.365043+00:00",
        "image": "https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/ayCrZLrNxfoWaS2jqRwP6C-2560-80.jpg",
        "full_content": "Is it a phone or a gimbal? This is Honor's new Robot Phone!\nWhether you're a creator or just someone who wants a smartphone camera with a difference, the Honor Robot Phone could surprise you.\nI LOVE my DJI Pocket 3, and since I bought it over two years ago, it has completely replaced two expensive full-frame cameras. Why? Convenience.\nWhen you’re trying to create content – especially for social media – convenience matters. The DJI Pocket 3 is much, much smaller than a full-frame camera, but it’s still another device that you have to carry. Yet its value and popularity show that the category has the potential to delight customers, whether or not they’ve used a gimbal.\nBuilding on this, the Honor Robot Phone takes smartphone cameras a huge step forward. Imagine the DJI Pocket 3 paired with a great smartphone camera like the Honor Magic 8 Pro, and you have one of the most unique smartphone cameras ever made. I went hands-on, and here’s why it’s so interesting.\nThe Honor Robot Phone is built for solo creators\nMost people who create content do NOT have a large team following and filming them.\nGimbals like the Pocket 3 have long featured a mode with facial recognition and object tracking, and the Honor Robot Phone is no different. Like my Pocket 3, it can lock itself onto a face – whether near or far – and pan and tilt as needed to ensure the subject remains front and center,\nThat’s a considerable achievement given that the gimbal component size is around 65% smaller than a regular gimbal, but the Honor Robot Phone also features a much larger viewfinder in the form of the big screen, and everything else that makes it a flagship smartphone.\nThis is further complemented by the case in the box, which comes with a magnetic kickstand attached that ensures you can stand the phone up and have the camera follow you wherever you are, no additional tripod needed.\nGet the latest news from Android Central, your trusted companion in the world of Android\nARRI helps your creative juices flow\nHowever, the Robot Phone also goes further in a way that surprised me: the partnership with ARRI, a designer of film and broadcast cameras for over 100 years.\nThe partnership doesn’t just improve the color science of all photos; it goes considerably further with a host of filters and effects designed to help you create the perfect look directly from the camera.\nThis is my first experience with ARRI, but I was quite impressed by the variety of options and the range of effects you can add while filming. You can also film entirely in LOG, and there's a range of preview filters in different styles, so you can see how your LOG footage will likely look after editing. The only downside is that you can't easily edit the filters after capture.\nThe upside? You get a gimbal built into a smartphone with a huge screen, which acts as a huge viewfinder.\nThe Robot Phone is also a flagship smartphone\nHere's what's easy to forget, at least when using the gimbal: the Honor Robot Phone is a flagship smartphone. That means you get everything that makes the best Android phones; Honor is primarily known for making smartphones, and the Robot Phone delivers the same experience as other flagship Honor phones.\nThe Pocket 3 doesn't offer the fastest menu navigation or the fastest feature changes, and even newer gimbals like the Pocket 4 and Insta 360 Luna aren't much faster. The Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 means the Robot Phone is quick to switch features and navigate menus, making it far more enjoyable to use as well.\nThen there's MagicOS, Honor’s software akin to Android 16. The Robot Phone is exclusive to China, so the software includes the Yoyo assistant and lacks Google services, but otherwise it's quite enjoyable to use.\nThe two other standout features\nThere are two features that make the Robot Phone stand out for me: the screen and the camera system.\nFirst, the 6.31-inch screen. It's smaller than most flagships, yet larger than every gimbal, but still ergonomically friendly given its size. It also has a 120Hz refresh rate, 4320Hz PWM dimming rate, and 6,800-nit peak brightness; in my brief time with it, the screen remained legible even under the harsh British afternoon sun.\nView Original\nView Original\nView Original\nView Original\nView Original\nView Original\nView Original\nView Original\nThe standout is the triple-camera setup. The 200MP main sensor has a smaller 1/1.28” sensor compared to the much larger 1-inch sensor found on the gimbal, and while this does show in footage, it's easily offset by the 200MP periscope telephoto — which offers 2.7x optical zoom and OIS — and the 50MP ultrawide. One of my biggest complaints about most gimbals is the lack of a true zoom, but the Robot Phone solves this.\nThe Robot Phone isn't perfect, but I really want one\nAfter spending a couple of hours with it, I had one key takeaway: the Honor Robot Phone isn't a perfect gimbal, nor is it a perfect smartphone, but I really want one.\nAs someone who likes filming with a gimbal for on-camera footage, as well as b-roll of products with far less success, the Robot Phone gives me the gimbal experience in a phone that's 9.6mm thick, which is far more pocket-friendly than the Pocket 3, which measures 33mm thick.\nAs the adage goes, the best camera is the one in your pocket, and the Robot Phone is arguably the best for creators because it offers something that no other phone does. Now, let's see if the Robot Phone eventually launches outside China.\nNirave is a veteran tech journalist and creator at House of Tech. He's reviewed over 1,000 phones and other consumer gadgets over the past 20 years. A heart attack at 33 inspired him to consider the Impact of Technology on our physical, mental, and emotional health. Say hi to him on Twitter or Threads\nYou must confirm your public display name before commenting\nPlease logout and then login again, you will then be prompted to enter your display name.",
        "content": "Is it a phone or a gimbal? This is Honor's new Robot Phone!\nWhether you're a creator or just someone who wants a smartphone camera with a difference, the Honor Robot Phone could surprise you.\nI LOVE my DJI Pocket 3, and since I bought it over two years ago, it has completely replaced two expensive full-frame cameras. Why? Convenience.\nWhen you’re trying to create content – especially for social me...",
