        "title": "Why Each Octopus Arm Has a Mind of Its Own",
        "link": "https://www.wired.com/story/octopus-arms-distributed-intelligence-nine-brains/",
        "source": "Science Latest",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.364398+00:00",
        "image": "https://media.wired.com/photos/6a7786527a4971962ec8627a/191:100/w_1280,c_limit/WIRED-OctopusBrain-HoiChan-SiteImage.jpg",
        "full_content": "Octopuses are clever enough to unscrew jars from the inside and throw sand at their annoying neighbors. But only a fraction of their smarts come from their doughnut-shaped brains. Over two-thirds of their roughly 500 million neurons reside in eight sucker-studded arms—a kind of distributed intelligence unique to cephalopods.\nEach of these powerful appendages can move in any direction and taste and feel with exquisite sensitivity. A nerve ring connecting all eight arms enables explorations of the seafloor that is not always governed by the central brain upstairs.\nWhat's it like when your arms have minds of their own? Many mysteries—anatomical, behavioral, and philosophical—remain. But here's some of what scientists have been able to learn about these unorthodox invertebrates.",
