    {
        "title": "Companies eye untapped gas in the 'Saudi Arabia of the Pacific'",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-14/the-papua-lng-project-near-australia-pacific-saudi-arabia/107028200",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.363571+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/683855adc86fafcd2fe3d3d677629884?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=490&cropW=871&xPos=0&yPos=42&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "The multi-billion-dollar LNG supply on Australia's doorstep\nGlobal oil and gas giants are rushing to green-light a massive LNG project on Australia's doorstep as war in the Middle East forces countries to look for alternative energy supplies.\nThe Elk-Antelope gas field in Papua New Guinea's Gulf Province contains the energy equivalent of more than 1 billion barrels of oil worth upwards of $70 billion.\nBut the site has remained untapped since its discovery in 2006.\nThe main proposal for the site, the Papua LNG project, would develop the area and build a 320-kilometre pipeline with the goal of producing more than 5 million tons of LNG per year.\nThe project has been beset with lengthy delays, from political upsets to cost blowouts, design overhauls, investor uncertainty and landowner disputes.\nIt has fuelled frustration among energy behemoths TotalEnergies, ExxonMobil and Santos, which all own significant stakes in the project.\nThe Papua New Guinean government has become increasingly desperate for investment as it struggles to get new resource projects up and running.\nAnd while one government minister says Papua LNG could be green-lit by the end of the year, experts and industry insiders say there are several hurdles it still needs to clear.\nAn untapped LNG supply\nThings looked radically different less than a year ago as Papua LNG's proponents were courting financiers.\nThe world was heading towards an LNG glut and some analysts deemed the project to be financially risky, especially in the absence of long-term supply agreements.\nBut things have changed dramatically.\nIranian missile strikes on facilities in Qatar, which supplied about a fifth of the world's LNG, have caused major supply disruptions that may last years.\nThis, combined with Iran's chokehold on the Strait of Hormuz, has sent energy customers searching for alternative LNG sources.\nEconomist Stephen Howes, the director of the Development Policy Centre at the Australian National University, said that could give Papua LNG the boost it needed.\n\"To have a source that's basically in the Asia-Pacific region, which is a stable region … is very attractive,\"\nJapan, China, South Korea and Taiwan are considered likely buyers.\nThe war has also raised the expectation of future LNG prices and delivered a windfall for companies like ExxonMobil, according to Professor Howes.\n\"They're cashed up because of these high prices and so they have more money to invest,\" he said.\nPNG looks to cash in\nFor Papua New Guinea, a resource-dependent country sometimes referred to as the \"Saudi Arabia of the Pacific\", Papua LNG will be an economic lifeline.\nProfessor Howes said the government was eager to get it up and running.\n\"To grow the economy, you need to have a constant flow of new projects every few years,\" he said.\n\"This is the longest that PNG has gone without a new resource project.\"\nSpeaking at an industry conference last month, petroleum minister Jimmy Maladina said he was confident a final investment decision would be reached by the end of the year, the final step to getting the project over the line.\n\"The ongoing conflict in the Middle East has reinforced the importance of energy security for nations around the world,\" he said.\n\"Countries are seeking reliable, stable and responsible supply of oil and gas and Papua New Guinea is well positioned to meet those demands.\"\nBut others remain sceptical.\nKevin Morrison, an analyst at the Institute for Energy Economics and Financial Analysis, said large quantities of new gas were still coming onto the market, particularly from the US.\n\"It's the biggest increase in LNG supply that the market has ever seen,\" he said.\nHe said the global supply disruptions and market volatility may also make customers more hesitant to sign long-term supply contracts.\nThat remains one major challenge for Papua LNG, which has not yet publicly announced any buyers.\nAn industry insider, who spoke with the ABC on the condition of anonymity, said there was \"deathly silence\" on the customer front.\n\"The Papua LNG Project needs customers. It does not have a project, let alone a project capable of being financed without them,\"\nBut, the insider said, the lack of announcements could be because sales and purchase agreements were not concluded or were being kept confidential.\nIt comes amid reports that TotalEnergies is looking to hand over its role as operator to ExxonMobil, which could result in cost reductions but also create delays.\nProject needs financial backers\nOn top of that, the venture still needs to secure financial backing.\nAlmost 30 major financial institutions have formally refused to bankroll the project after several civil society groups alleged it violated six of the 10 Equator Principles.\nThe Equator Principles are an internationally recognised benchmark for financial institutions to determine, assess and manage environmental and social risks.\nA complaint filed by Asia-Pacific advocacy group Jubilee Australia on behalf of the civil society groups raised concerns about human rights, biodiversity, climate change and project transparency.\nIt said affected Indigenous communities lacked adequate information about the project, its risks and their options and rights under international law.\nThe complaint also said the project area was home to 100 species that were new to science or had not been scientifically described.\n\"It is not possible to understand, let alone mitigate, risks to individual species or their impact on critical habitats if science has never studied them,\" Jubilee Australia said in a statement.\nIn response to the complaint, TotalEnergies said it was committed to respecting the Equator Principles and was working with authorities and communities to ensure the project exceeded social and environmental standards.\nThe company said it had adopted a grassroots consultative approach utilising village liaison officers and third-party legal advisors to share and discuss information about the project with impacted communities, including vulnerable groups.\nIn a statement posted online, the company said the consultation included discussions about training and employment opportunities, local business development and social investment activities.\nA policy centred around preserving biodiversity and ecosystems was also being developed, the company said, announcing that the project aimed to deliver \"biodiversity net gain\".\nPNG villagers have mixed feelings\nAs well as securing customers and financial backing, Papua LNG also needs support from affected landowners in the Gulf Province to move forward.\nHundreds of landowners from the area gathered in Port Moresby this week to negotiate benefit-sharing arrangements — a crucial step in getting the project over the line.\nBut several Indigenous landowners told the ABC they had mixed feelings about Papua LNG.\nMeg Haeko from Mareke village said people were excited about the benefits and development opportunities it could bring to their extremely remote communities.\n\"We want basic services like roads, hospitals and schools. That is something our people are looking forward [to]. It will change their life,\" she said.\nBut she was worried about the impacts of the project and how they would be addressed.\n\"How well can they protect and minimise the risk that is involved during the construction phase, after the construction and the decommissioning part of it?\" she said.\n\"The developer hasn't given enough information about what they're going to do.\"\nJoseph Irae from Paivara and Maerava villages said he did not fully understand the project and how it would impact his people.\nHe also said his community did not know enough about the project to enter into an agreement with Papua LNG.\n\"It's a very sad thing,\" he said.\n\"The interested stakeholder in this project must come and talk to the people. Not just a couple of hours talking … you have to spend week or night, mingle with the people to know them.\"\nHis biggest concern was the environment.\n\"The environment speaks to the people,\" he said.\n\"People listen to the environment. We survive from it. We learn from it. We live with it. And when we die, we go back to the land.\"",
        "content": "The multi-billion-dollar LNG supply on Australia's doorstep\nGlobal oil and gas giants are rushing to green-light a massive LNG project on Australia's doorstep as war in the Middle East forces countries to look for alternative energy supplies.\nThe Elk-Antelope gas field in Papua New Guinea's Gulf Province contains the energy equivalent of more than 1 billion barrels of oil worth upwards of $70 bill...",
