    {
        "title": "Everybody wants to cut migration but nobody wants to say how",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-14/everybody-wants-to-cut-migration-but-nobody-wants-to-say-how/107034230",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.363569+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/a0237e9d9b7798cc1228c51d075b821b?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=805&cropW=1431&xPos=0&yPos=66&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "analysis\nWhen Tony Burke, regarded as one of the smoothest operators in the ministry, hastily withdrew from a press club speech about migration last week — so late that tickets to the sellout event had to be refunded — tongues were set wagging: something must be up.\nAs the ABC then reported, something was up: after much back and forth, the cabinet could not agree on details of the sweeping migration cuts he was set to announce.\nMinisters insisted discussions were civil and productive, but at the time of writing they were still going.\nNobody could have imagined that, within days, it would be relegated to only the second-strangest frontbencher-induced migration-related press club kerfuffle of the month.\nEnter Andrew Bragg, the Liberal housing spokesperson, who took to the esteemed stage on Wednesday.\nAmong the many, many things he said in a speech his colleague delicately described as \"just his opinion\", there was this on migration:\n\"[A] reduction can be achieved while benefiting the budget and the economy, including by … modest reductions in international students, working holiday makers, and post-study work visas.\"\nIt sounded like some meat on the bones of Angus Taylor's policy to link migration levels to housing construction. Here at last was some detail on which migration streams would be trimmed to meet the Coalition's target.\nExcept it wasn't. Bragg was riffing, or as he put it, trying to \"move the debate on towards where the numbers are probably going to hit\".\nThe visa categories had not been green-lit by the shadow cabinet, and in fact some in the shadow cabinet vehemently disagreed.\nMatt Canavan, the Nationals leader, insisted that his party would not allow any cuts to backpackers on working holiday visas, who were \"not the problem\" and were needed in the regions. Taylor said no details were final and Bragg had been pulled for a quiet chat.\nLabor, the Liberals and the Nationals — all in a muddle on migration.\nIt sounds like perfect political fodder for One Nation, which has built its political insurgency on the premise that it is the only party capable of \"plain speaking\" on migration.\nExcept that Pauline Hanson's party may be even more muddled than the others.\nIts \"target\" of 130,000 was at first a temporary migration target, or possibly temporary and permanent; it is now permanent, but only temporarily. Got that?\nThere is a reason this stuff is hard, even for someone whose desire to cut migration is as unrepentant as Hanson's: for all the focus on the level of migration, its causes are murky, and it is harder for politicians to control than they would like to admit.\nThe migration net\nFor starters, the main figure everyone seems determined to talk about — net overseas migration (or \"the NOM\") — is almost universally misunderstood.\nFor one thing, it is not actually a measure of \"migrants\" at all, at least in the way they are conventionally understood in the political debate — or at least not just migrants.\nIt tracks arrivals and departures of both foreigners and Australians, excluding short-term visits.\nAnd because it measures inflows and outflows, it is not a reliable measure of how many migrants are actually in Australia at any one time. It measures speed, not mileage.\nSo you can add successive numbers together to say that well over a million people have entered Australia in net terms since Labor took office — as its opponents do — but this gives you no historical context and only indirectly tells you about the migration system.\nIt does not tell you, for instance, that net migration was not just low but actually negative during the pandemic.\nMore people were leaving than were arriving, as the border shut and many migrants returned home. When the borders reopened, the pent-up demand was one cause of the spike, as were pandemic policies encouraging migrants who stayed here to stay even longer.\nThis doesn't 'debunk' the popular perception that migration is unusually high — if you look at the total number of visas on issue, you will see that that number is also high.\nBut it does lead to confusion now that net migration has become the main fixation of political debate: every party is scrambling to frame their policies in terms of what it will mean for the NOM, but NOM targets are only loosely related to actual visas.\nEven if a political party can nominate the visa streams it wants to clamp down on — as Labor seems very close to doing — it still cannot be assured of delivering a particular NOM, as there will always be comings and goings (including of Australians) outside its control.\nLabor: half-target, half-forecast\nLabor's apparent solution will be to set a plan to meet the level of NOM that Treasury forecasts will occur in the year 2027-28, which is currently 225,000.\nThis is challenging enough, given that Treasury forecasts have generally been exceeded in recent years.\nBut it is not quite a target, even if it sounds like one.\nLabor's actual target seems to be the number of people on so-called \"bridging\" visas, an assortment of visas given to those who are in between recognised categories like student or humanitarian visas.\nCabinet discussions have highlighted the runaway growth of the bridging visa cohort, which numbers more than 400,000 — a level roughly double the pre-pandemic norms.\nMany of the people in that bucket are likely those described by the former top public servant Martin Parkinson as \"permanently temporary\" in his often-forgotten report to the Albanese government in 2023.\nThe term describes those who have outstayed their initial reason for coming to Australia and are unlikely to seek or be granted permanent residency, but who may yet stay for many years, either on bridging visas, as temporary workers, or as perennial students.\nLabor will seek to reduce that number with measures that may include limiting work rights for those who are on bridging visas while they appeal rejected humanitarian claims, and those who are on them because they have applied for family visas.\nBoth measures are contentious but might, as one minister suggested to the ABC, salvage something of \"the spirit\" of Parkinson's review by clarifying the status of those who are in visa limbo.\nThe measures would also be expected to reduce the NOM, as would a likely measure to restrict the length of stay for some backpackers.\nBut the government still could not say with certainty that any of this would achieve a NOM of 225,000.\nThe Coalition: a NOM target we cannot know\nThe Coalition's target also has an element of fuzziness.\nIt has promised a one-to-one ratio between housing completions and net migration, but has yet to nominate any visa categories and has given itself some wriggle room to change that ratio in future.\nWhile Andrew Bragg used current completion rates to suggest an indicative NOM target of under 180,000, the Coalition will not actually have to nail itself to any specific number before the election, since by definition the future NOM will not be known until after it.\nThis has caused some consternation, with former Liberal staffer Peta Credlin telling Taylor in an interview this week that he would \"still need a number\" if Labor and One Nation were going to set them.\nThat prompted Taylor to make the curious suggestion that because housing completions were so dismally low under Labor — a bad thing — that would likely mean a housing target of even further below the 180,000 than Bragg suggests — a good thing.\nThe Nationals' opposition to any backpacker cuts is just one of the problems that will face Taylor when he is eventually required to be more specific.\nBusiness concerns over skilled workers and community concerns over family visas are another.\nOne Nation: possibly no NOM target\nBut even Hanson has seemed reluctant to nominate visas for the chopping block. Her party has the most ambiguous policies of all.\nOne Nation's website says the party's policy involves \"capping visas at 130,000 per year\" and that this would \"cut immigration by over 570,000 people from current Labor levels\".\nThough Hanson last week said that the 130,000 was for permanent visas, it is far from clear that this squared with that language.\nIf 130,000 is a 'cut' of 570,000, the implication is that whatever overall figure the website suggests is 'current' is 700,000.\nThat cannot be the permanent visa program, which is just shy of 200,000. It cannot be net migration either, which peaked at 555,000 in 2023 and is now closer to 300,000.\nIt could be migrant arrivals (ignoring departures), which peaked at about 700,000 in 2023 but are now lower. Or it could be some combination of temporary and permanent.\nWhatever it is, One Nation has not stated which visa streams would be subject to its 130,000 target.\nWhen asked that question, Hanson tends to point to the 75,000 people she says are here \"illegally\" and can be deported \"immediately\".\nBut the Department of Home Affairs says that figure is also an elusive one, and that its members are changing all the time as they actually leave the country.\nWhile it does contain \"genuine\" overstayers, it is best thought of as a \"waiting room\" containing many people who are preparing to leave at any given time.\nAll of this leaves One Nation's NOM target unclear.\nAnd while most voters will, for good reason, not engage with this dizzying array of numbers and visa streams, for those who wish to govern, they matter.",
        "content": "analysis\nWhen Tony Burke, regarded as one of the smoothest operators in the ministry, hastily withdrew from a press club speech about migration last week — so late that tickets to the sellout event had to be refunded — tongues were set wagging: something must be up.\nAs the ABC then reported, something was up: after much back and forth, the cabinet could not agree on details of the sweeping migrati...",
