        "title": "Small businesses lacking skills 'don't know if you're turning a profit'",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-14/taskforce-launched-to-equip-small-business-financial-literacy/107016448",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.363556+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/859c1ab42ccf00fb470f92eb14239da9?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=759&cropW=1350&xPos=0&yPos=135&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "National taskforce launches to improve financial literacy rates for small business owners\nIn short:\nUp to a third of small business owners do not know if they made a profit last month because they struggle with financial literacy, according to new data.\nIn this article:\nWhen Storm Menzies first launched her small business a year ago, she expected the product design process to be her biggest challenge.\n\"That's like a tiny part of running a business; that's the fun part,\" she said.\n\"All the financial stuff and the actual running of the business, I had no experience doing that.\"\nThe small business owner based in Newcastle designs attachments for cosmetics to make the products more accessible for people.\nBut with no previous business knowledge and a lack of reliable tools available for her to learn the ropes, she turned to platforms like YouTube for help.\n\"The financial element is so important, but you are kind of going like 'where do I get support for this?'\" she said.\n\"A lot of trial and error, a lot of mistakes … for me, I felt very overwhelmed.\"\nOther small business owners are having the same experience, with research from accounting platform Xero suggesting more than one third do not know if they made a profit last month.\nWith more than 2.6 million small businesses in Australia as of August last year, Xero's managing director Angad Soin said that has a broader economic impact.\n\"If you're running a small business and you don't know if you're turning a profit, you don't know if you can pay yourself a wage,\" he said.\n\"That has an economic impact on everyone because small businesses also usually buy and sell from other small businesses, so if you're not paying yourself a wage, they're not putting money back into the economy.\n\"If they're not able to know if they're turning a profit, they don't know if they can hire that next person, keeping people on staff.\"\nLoading...\nTackling a lack of financial literacy\nTo help address the issue, a parliamentary task force has been established to look at how to better equip firms with the skills to run a business.\nRepresentatives from small business, finance, education, and politics met with Assistant Treasurer Daniel Mulino and Small Business Minister Anne Aly in Parliament House on Thursday to discuss how they can support an industry that contributes $623 billion to the economy every year.\n\"Government needs to work together with business, with advisors, with technology providers, with community, to ensure that small businesses have all the conditions that they need, not just to get off the ground, but also to access all the resources that they can as well,\" Dr Aly said.\nSkye Cappuccio from the Council of Small Business Organisations said developing those resources was essential for supporting current and future business owners.\n\"It's not a fault of any individual small business owner, but we don't have the established systems and structures to support that learning across the country,\" she said.\nThose resources could also contribute to smaller firms staying open for business, with data from CreditorWatch indicating that about 12 per cent of businesses are forced to close after three years.\nIn the 2024-2025 financial year, 370,500 businesses closed, a number that was offset by 437,150 new businesses that opened.\nVerity Hare, who supports women running trade businesses, said improving financial literacy would also help alleviate stress in other areas for business owners.\n\"That cash flow stress or that lack of financial knowledge when things go kind of south with finances puts a lot of strain on relationships and mental health kind of gets affected, and it's a huge flow-on effect,\" she said.\nStepping towards solutions\nOver the coming months, the task force will conduct several workshops, and there are already ideas about how businesses can be supported earlier in their career.\n\"[Looking] at how we educate people in some of their entry-level training programs and even at the level of secondary school,\" Ms Cappuccio said.\nFor Storm Menzies, she would like to see more tools and greater collaboration to create a more level playing field and help more people launch their own small businesses regardless of their experience.\n\"You see a really stark contrast between people who have grown up with financial literacy, people who may have parents who have run businesses … [and then] people who may not have had the experience with finances and the family support,\" she said.\n\"It feels quite shameful, particularly when you're around other startups who are raising and earning all this money, and you feel almost ashamed of being like 'well, I literally don't know what you're talking about'.\n\"I think more mentoring and more publicly available programs … [to] actually learn and build and grow your self-confidence, which I think is a really big part of starting a business, actually believing that you can and believing that you have the capacity to learn what you need to keep that business going.\"",
        "content": "National taskforce launches to improve financial literacy rates for small business owners\nIn short:\nUp to a third of small business owners do not know if they made a profit last month because they struggle with financial literacy, according to new data.\nIn this article:\nWhen Storm Menzies first launched her small business a year ago, she expected the product design process to be her biggest challe...",
