        "full_content": "Report confirms cause of deadly California wildfire that killed 19\nIn the wake of a devastating 2025 wildfire, Victor Shaw's family found his body by the side of the road on their California street.\nThe 66-year-old had died with a garden hose still in his hand, trying to protect the home his family had lived in for more than five decades.\nHis sister, Shari Shaw, had tried to convince him to evacuate with her when the fire approached on January 7.\nHer brother told her he wanted to stay behind to try to save the house.\nAs she turned back and called his name, their home was already going up in flames. He did not answer.\nMr Shaw was one of 19 people ultimately killed in the blaze dubbed the Eaton fire, one of several which swept through California in early January 2025.\nEighteen months later, officials say the fire, which burned over 5,600 hectares and destroyed close to 10,000 structures, was started by sparks coming off a transmission tower.\nApproximately 12 seconds after those sparks were seen, according to investigators, the fire ignited.\nArson investigations are continuing into the other fires, which formed one of the most destructive wildfire seasons the state has ever seen.\n'Mountain of flame' swept through northern LA\nThe Eaton fire broke out on January 7, 2025, at about 6:18pm local time.\nWithin 10 minutes, the blaze had spread from the electrical tower to cover the entire mountainside, according to residents nearby.\n\"It was just a mountain of flame,\" Michael Olson, a 70-year-old living opposite the tower in Pasadena, told the LA Times.\n\"We left home thinking we'd never see it again.\"\nThe area was part of the Angeles National Forest, located in northern LA County, with major highways to the west and east.\nThe powerful Santa Ana winds swept through the mountain, with gusts of up to 129 kilometres per hour.\nIn January 2025, it was covered in dense shrubs, scrubland and small trees, and there had been no rain for more than half a year.\nIt was a tinderbox.\nLoading...\nIn the following days the fire would continue to grow and spread quickly, not being fully contained until January 31.\nBy then the investigation was already well underway to determine the cause.\nVideo showed 'bright blue and white flashes' from tower\nA report published last week placed blame on a transmission tower on the hillside belonging to utility company Southern California Edison (SCE).\nThe company had previously acknowledged its likely involvement in the cause of the blaze.\nLead investigator and Los Angeles fire captain Rick Yartz said video footage showed two \"electrical arcing events in quick succession\".\n\"[This led] to an unknown burning material falling from the SCE tower's location into the dry receptive fuel bed below,\" he wrote in the report.\nTwo \"bright blue and white flashes\" were seen, he added.\n\"An orange glow [was] seen as if it were dripping fire or glowing material from the bright blue and white flashes.\n\"When the second flash happens [in the video], more glowing orange material drips and falls towards the ground.\"\nOther witnesses gave fire investigators more videos, photos, and even sketches of what they had seen up on the ridge near their homes.\n\"The totality of the information obtained during this investigation … leads me to the conclusion that the cause of this fire is electrical in nature,\" Mr Yartz wrote in his report.\nSouthern California Edison spokeswoman Kathleen Dunleavy said the company had taken its role in the fire \"seriously from the beginning\".\nIn October 2025, the company established a compensation fund for victims even before fire investigators had published their final report.\nTo date, it has committed hundreds of millions of dollars in compensation.\nIn lawsuits earlier this year, it claimed Los Angeles officials failed to send evacuation notices to residents in east and west Altadena in time to escape the fire.\nAlleged arsonist set to face second trial for Palisades blaze\nAt the same time as the Eaton fire was burning through Altadena, the Palisades fire was tearing through the Pacific Palisades, Topanga and Malibu.\nThat fire ultimately killed 12 people and destroyed almost 7,000 structures, making it the second-most destructive wildfire in Los Angeles history.\nThe man accused of starting the blaze is 30-year-old Jonathan Rinderknecht, who was hiking a trail above Los Angeles on New Year's Eve, 2024.\nThe Palisades fire smouldered for a week in the area before intense winds whipped it into the deadly blaze.\nProsecutors argued Mr Rinderknecht, an Uber driver living in North Hollywood, had become resentful of the wealthy residents in the Pacific Palisades.\nHe was accused of starting the fire as \"societal revenge\", during a federal trial in June this year.\nHis defence team argued the fire had been started by New Year's fireworks.\nThey also alleged the scene of the fire was left unprotected for almost two weeks, adding no video had caught the moment of ignition or identified the exact time it happened.\nBut a jury was unable to reach a verdict, leading a Los Angeles federal judge to declare a mistrial.\nA new trial date has been set for October.\n'The 2026 fire year will challenge us'\nCalifornia is already at the peak of its 2026 wildfire season.\nSince the start of August, CAL Fire has recorded 28 incidents and almost 11,000 hectares burned.\nThe largest of those fires, the 4,198-hectare Gann fire, has been burning across Calaveras County since August 3, and is only 72 per cent contained.\nFire danger is predicted to be above normal for August for north-eastern California, according to the US National Interagency Fire Center.\n\"Drought developed and/or intensified in portions of the Northwest, south-east California, northern New Mexico, and central Colorado,\" the agency said in its last predictions.\nPersistent drought and increased vegetation growth were noted as factors in raising the fire risk across California.\nUS Forest Service chief Tom Schultz issued a statement earlier this year, saying the agency was positioned for a \"strong, safe, and effective\" year.\n\"Predictive services indicate the 2026 fire year will challenge us,\"\n- Share options\n- Copy link\n- X (formerly Twitter)",
