        "title": "Hospital missed critical signs before young dad's death, review finds",
        "link": "https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-14/mackay-base-hospital-mena-tuibua-death-review/106993430",
        "source": "Just In",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.363537+00:00",
        "image": "https://live-production.wcms.abc-cdn.net.au/150f9f21e0c8d2ec1ae32c245b3491b4?impolicy=wcms_watermark_news&cropH=810&cropW=1440&xPos=0&yPos=49&width=862&height=485&imformat=generic",
        "full_content": "Critical mistakes made at Mackay Base Hospital before father's death, review finds\nThere were no free beds or even an empty treatment bay in the emergency department on the night youth worker Amenatave \"Mena\" Tuibua walked into Mackay Base Hospital with a sharp pain in his chest.\nThe public hospital was at capacity on the night of May 26, 2025. Patients waiting for beds were stuck in the emergency department and others were ramped in ambulances.\nMr Tuibua, 41, sat in the emergency department waiting room with no cardiac monitoring for three hours before he was sent home.\nHe left unaware doctors had failed to correctly read his ECG, which showed an abnormal heart rhythm, and had failed to follow guidelines for assessing cardiac pain.\nLater it was judged \"highly probable\" that medical staff had looked at another patient's blood test result instead of his and therefore missed a tell-tale sign of heart damage.\nThe father of four was leading a youth camp on Whitsunday Island three weeks later when he collapsed in front of students. He was unable to be revived.\nMr Tuibua's wife, Shey Tuibua, was at rugby training with their son in Mackay when she was told her husband had a heart attack.\n\"He died the day before our 17th anniversary,\" she said.\n\"The helicopters flew in and just couldn't get him, couldn't save him.\n\"The moment your life goes from being awesome to a living nightmare.\"\nFalling through 'the cracks'\nAn internal review ordered by Mackay Hospital and Health Service (MHHS) found Mr Tuibua was not treated as a high-risk patient despite his history of high blood pressure and a family history of cardiac problems.\nThe root cause analysis (RCA) probe into his treatment said several clinical findings \"fell through the cracks on a very busy evening\".\nIt said \"highly suspicious\" changes to his heart's electrical activity were missed and that it was \"highly probable\" a clinician looked at another patient's blood test results instead of Mr Tuibua's.\nA critical blood test for troponin, a marker that shows damage to heart muscle, was not reviewed until the next morning, long after Mr Tuibua went home.\nA doctor, who had not treated Mr Tuibua, observed the elevated troponin level result but did not raise this with a senior doctor.\nThe review also found chest pain guidelines, which would have informed the doctors' treatment plan, were not adequately followed.\nAdditionally, a \"statement of attendance\" to take to a GP for follow-up care was not issued.\n\"It cannot be discounted that excessive clinical workload, clinician fatigue and environmental challenges have impacted clinical assessment, timely investigations, and contributed to the risk for human error,\" the reviewers said.\nMs Tuibua said the analysis raised questions about how often mistakes were happening in Mackay's health service.\n\"We deserve a safe and well-run [emergency department] in this town and, right now, I feel like it's not,\" she said.\n\"My kids have gone through hell and back. The young people he worked with … they're all having to grieve because of human error.\"\nBed shortage contributes to 'poor patient outcomes'\nThe RCA document says the overcrowded hospital may have contributed to other deaths and patient harm.\n\"Multiple Severity Assessment Code (SAC1) incident reviews have identified access block to right treatment in the right place at the right time as a contributor to a poor outcome,\" it said.\nQueensland Health's website defines SAC1 incidents as those that include death or likely permanent harm that are not reasonably expected as an outcome of health care.\nMHHS did not respond to the ABC's questions about how many avoidable deaths and permanent-harm outcomes had been caused by bed shortages.\nIn a statement, chief executive Sean Birgan said hospital representatives had met with Mr Tuibua's family to offer a \"sincere apology\".\nHe said the health service was continuing to offer support, but was unable to make further comments due to patient confidentiality.\nHowever, the MHHS said it had seen an 11 per cent increase in urgent presentations to its ED from July 2025 to May 2026, and that 80 per cent of patients were seen within recommended time frames.\nIt said the review of Mr Tuibua's case had resulted in improvements to several procedures.\nIn a statement, the Queensland Coroners Court confirmed it had reopened an investigation into Mr Tuibua's death.\nIt said the Far Northern Coroner was awaiting \"information from a Queensland statutory body\".\nPressure across Queensland\nHealth Consumers Queensland's Keith Tracey-Patte said he was worried more mistakes were occurring because of growing pressure on the health system.\n\"What we're seeing here in Mackay, like we are seeing across the rest of the state, is real pressure that's put on the staff and the patients in a really complex environment,\" he said.\n\"It's really hard to grow the workforce to make sure you've got enough people available with the right skills to deliver the care that people need.\"\nThe planned 128-bed expansion of Mackay Base Hospital has not started despite initial promises to have the work complete by late 2026.\nThe expansion was announced in 2022 but an independent review revealed the project was underfunded and that a new master plan was needed to ensure it met the needs of the community.\nOn Tuesday, the Queensland government unveiled the new plans for the hospital upgrade, with the completion of 128 beds and a new clinical services building set down for 2030.\n'Mena' remembered\nViliame Tuibua, 13, said the loss of his dad had \"ruined him\", but he and his sister, Resina, had relied on their Christian faith and community support over the past year.\n\"It felt like I was just in this unescapable void,\" he said.\n\"I'm getting there … I've been playing a lot of touch [footy] and going to new places. It's given me a spark of happiness.\"\nMr Tuibua's family remembers him as an \"extraordinary\" member of the community and a \"kind and caring\" father.\nThis year, Mackay State High School announced plans to honour his youth work through the Mena Cup, a rugby carnival to be played each year on the anniversary of his death.\nResina, 14, said she would always remember her dad's generosity.\n\"I just know he was such a good person that I just, in any situation, I think, 'What would dad do?'\" she said.",
