        "title": "Suno Studio 2.0 Turns Played MIDI Into a Prompt for AI-Generated Audio",
        "link": "https://www.unite.ai/suno-studio-2-0-turns-played-midi-into-a-prompt-for-ai-generated-audio/",
        "source": "Unite.AI",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.927939+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.unite.ai/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/suno-studio-2-0-turns-played-midi-into-a-prompt-for-ai-generated-audio.jpg",
        "full_content": "AI Models & Platforms\nSuno Studio 2.0 Turns Played MIDI Into a Prompt for AI-Generated Audio\nAdd Unite.AI to your preferred sources on Google\nSuno released Studio 2.0 on August 13, 2026, a major upgrade to its browser-based generative audio workstation that adds MIDI recording and editing, a wavetable synth, a session-aware chat collaborator, custom user-built effects plugins, track automation, and higher-fidelity stem separation. The update, detailed in a post by Studio product manager Henry Phipps, is available now to Premier subscribers and pushes the tool further toward the shape of a conventional digital audio workstation, with AI generation woven into each standard feature rather than standing apart from them.\nMIDI support is the centerpiece. Phipps called it the most-requested addition to Studio since the workstation launched in September 2025. Users can now import, record, and edit MIDI directly on the timeline, play notes with a typing keyboard if they do not own a controller, and design sounds with a new built-in wavetable synth. The distinctive piece is what Studio does with that MIDI: clips can serve as the prompt for new audio generations, so a producer can play in a chord progression or melody and have Suno render it as audio, extend it, or build on it.\nWhat Studio 2.0 Adds Beyond MIDI\nThe new chat bar, in beta at launch, ties a conversational assistant into the live session. Suno describes it as a collaborator that understands music production: users can ask it to create instruments and vocals from scratch, generate lyrics or parts in the context of the current project, or tidy up a session. The same chat interface is also the mechanism for building custom audio plugins. Beyond the factory devices, which include sidechain compression and convolution reverb, users can design their own effects from scratch by describing them in chat. Suno said plugin creation will not require credits at launch, though a credit structure may be introduced later.\nAutomation arrives for the first time, letting users draw curves that alter track and plugin parameters across an arrangement. An updated stem-separation tool extracts component parts of a song at what Suno calls amazing audio fidelity, including audio brought in from outside Suno, which can then be split, arranged, and rearranged inside Studio. Premier subscribers can export 32-bit/48kHz multitracks and stems without limitations.\nThe framing throughout the announcement is deliberate. Phipps wrote that text prompts work well for finding inspiration but are no substitute for playing a part on a MIDI keyboard or recording with a mic or analog instrument, and said the company spent the past year watching artists work and built Studio 2.0 directly from that feedback. The stated design conclusion: the familiar machinery of pre-AI music production (guitars, drum machines, synths, effects, step sequencers) should exist inside the next generation of music software, Studio included.\nA Product Moving Toward the Industry It Sued-Over\nStudio 2.0 lands one day after Suno announced a global partnership with BMG, one of the largest music companies, tied to what Suno says will be its first music model developed with the music industry. That deal promises new economic opportunities for artists and songwriters who opt in, and deeper collaboration on Suno’s programs for independent artists. It follows a principles post on August 6, 2026 in which CEO Mikey Shulman laid out the company’s positions: no artist names in training metadata, no prompts for specific artists or copyrighted songs, third-party screening of uploads, and coming audio watermarking and fingerprinting so Suno-generated songs can be identified on other platforms.\nThe sequencing matters because Suno spent the past two years as the music industry’s primary litigation target. The major labels sued the company in 2024 over its training data, and the fight has been running since; Unite.AI covered Suno’s post-lawsuit principles reset earlier this month, and the parallel label campaign against rival Udio. A Warner Music Group partnership arrived in November 2025. The BMG deal, the principles, and now a workstation built around conventional production workflows read as one continuous argument: Suno wants to be infrastructure the industry uses, not a generator the industry sues.\nWhere Studio 2.0 Sits Among Production Tools\nThe feature list now overlaps meaningfully with entry-level digital audio workstations: multitrack timeline editing, MIDI, automation, effects, and export. What it does not do is run third-party plugins — the synthesizer and effects are Suno’s own, whether factory-built or chat-generated — which keeps Studio a closed environment rather than a host for the existing plugin ecosystem producers already own.\nThe pricing gate is the other boundary. Studio 2.0 is Premier-tier only, the same tier the original Studio launched to last year, so the DAW-style workflow is reserved for Suno’s highest-paying subscribers. Suno has also separately flagged changes to its downloads policy taking effect September 3, 2026, aimed at limiting mass distribution of generated songs to streaming platforms — a constraint that sits alongside the export freedoms Studio 2.0 grants paying producers.\nSuno’s near-term roadmap is now visible on two tracks: the opt-in, industry-developed music model promised under the BMG partnership, and the continued conversion of Studio from a generator with editing bolted on into the production environment those licensed models would run inside.",
        "content": "AI Models & Platforms\nSuno Studio 2.0 Turns Played MIDI Into a Prompt for AI-Generated Audio\nAdd Unite.AI to your preferred sources on Google\nSuno released Studio 2.0 on August 13, 2026, a major upgrade to its browser-based generative audio workstation that adds MIDI recording and editing, a wavetable synth, a session-aware chat collaborator, custom user-built effects plugins, track automation, an...",
