        "title": "Unsealed documents reveal details of RCMP's response, investigation into Tumbler Ridge shooting",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/british-columbia/tumbler-ridge-unsealed-documents-9.7305135?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.926223+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/7e0a8524-fb1e-4bf7-ba4c-945ae3bd6808,1771022102338/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C194%2C6000%2C3375%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "Unsealed documents reveal details of RCMP's response, investigation into Tumbler Ridge shooting\nRCMP's minute-by-minute response to the shooting is made public – though critical details remain redacted\nDocuments unsealed through efforts by CBC News and a consortium of media outlets reveal new details of the RCMP's response and investigation into the February 2026 mass shooting in Tumbler Ridge, B.C.\nThe documents, which include search warrants, production orders and sealing orders, provide a snapshot of a complex investigation, laying out for the first time law enforcement's account of the day shots rang out at Tumbler Ridge Secondary School.\nThe newly unredacted files from the RCMP paint a picture of how front-line officers experienced the events of that day.\nSignificant portions of the documents remain redacted, including timestamps crucial to the chronology, such as how long it took officers to enter the school after receiving reports of shots fired.\nThe documents say that on Feb. 10, 2026, RCMP responded to an active shooter at the high school in Tumbler Ridge and found six deceased victims in a school littered with spent shell casings. They identify the shooter as 18-year-old Jesse Van Rootselaar, who was found dead in the school with self-inflicted gunshot wounds.\nSearch warrants filed by the RCMP describe the evidence, physical and electronic, that police identified as they attempted to piece together what happened that day.\nOne sealing order filed in early March describes concern that releasing records connected to the investigation could compromise future public inquests and pose a risk to the public.\n911 calls reported erratic driver, shots fired\nThe mass shooting, one of the worst in Canadian history, shook the small northern B.C. community, and is now the subject of a coroner's inquest investigating the role that mental health, access to weapons, and artificial intelligence played in the tragedy.\nThroughout the documents, Van Rootselaar is referred to by her last name, full name, \"Jesse,\" and \"the shooter.\"\nA production and sealing order sworn by Const. Jonathan Paquin of the RCMP North District Major Crime Unit on March 6 describes a flurry of 911 calls received the morning that chaos unfolded in Tumbler Ridge.\nOn Feb. 10, RCMP received a call about \"an SUV that had been driving erratically from [redacted], which is the residence of Jesse.\"\nThe vehicle driven to the school was later identified as a green 2023 Hyundai Palisade.\nA caller reported that \"there were two dead bodies and shotgun shells on the floor\" and they \"thought that the eldest son was armed and had left, and that the mother and son were dead.\"\nRCMP advised the caller to leave the scene untouched, close the door and return to their residence.\nRCMP then received multiple calls of shots fired at the secondary school.\nThe documents include a minute-by-minute accounting of various officers arriving on the scene, hearing shots fired, entering the school, and locating deceased victims and the deceased shooter.\n\"Police were shot at by the suspect which caused them to hold while being shot at,\" it reads in part.\nBecause timestamps are redacted from the documents, it's unknown how much time elapsed between the events described over several pages.\nFor example, the documents do not relay how much time passed between the initial 911 call describing the erratic vehicle, and police entering the school. This week, CBC News and a consortium of other media have been in court to argue that some of the redacted information be made public.\nThe production order also reveals confusion over whether there was a second shooter in the school, possibly because the shooter went by the aliases Jesse Jacobs and Jesse Strain, in addition to Jesse Van Rootselaar.\nOne inspector identified the shooter as the \"eldest child in the STRAIN/JACOBS family, and that they were obsessed with guns,\" the document reads.\n\"[The] inspector reported that a possible second shooter was Jesse VAN ROOTSELAAR,\" reads the document, though it's later detailed that police identified these were the same person.\nDetails of firearms remain sealed\nA search warrant filed the day after the shooting describes evidence sought by police, including \"human biological substances including but not limited to: blood, hair, fingerprints and human tissue,\" \"firearms, ammunition, spent cartridge casings,\" and \"computers, cell phones or tablets, that may belong to Jesse Van Rootselaar.\"\nA production order lists physical evidence located inside Van Rootselaar's home, including a Sony Handycam, a black iPhone with a cracked screen inside a black case, a purple Aocwei laptop, a MacBook Air, an SD card and a purple digital camera.\nA search warrant filed on Feb. 11 describes the scene at Van Rootselaar's home. It says two RCMP corporals observed \"a shotgun [redacted] that was unloaded as well as a pellet gun\" and \"insecure firearms inside the residence.\"\nBetween Feb. 13 and 20, a firearms tracing investigation with the Canadian Firearms Program attempted to trace four firearms of those seized from the secondary school and a second, redacted address. The details of the firearms are redacted, though two shotguns and a rifle are listed.\nElectronic records sought\nA section of the order describes a Police Records Information Management Environment — or PRIME — review of the teen.\nAny individual who interacts with law enforcement in B.C., from witnesses to suspects, is entered into PRIME's centralized database, regardless of the nature of the interaction.\nThe order says Van Rootselaar was carded to 12 PRIME files, dating back to April 2018 when she would have been around 10 years old. The details of those incidents are redacted.\nA section about Van Rootselaar's social media accounts is redacted in its entirety, with nearly seven pages of text fully blacked out.\nBut the file says on Feb. 15, 2026, RCMP were notified by the FBI that OpenAI had located two ChatGPT accounts believed to be used by Van Rootselaar. Details of those accounts are also redacted.\n\"Jesse's ChatGPT account was subject to an internal review in June 2025 after Jesse's chat conversation discussed matters that could be related to the school shooting,\" it said.\n\"OpenAI banned Jesse's ChatGPT account and did not notify police at that time.\"\nA request to seal\nOne document, an application for an order to seal filed March 6 by Paquin, shows that police were concerned that unsealing the production order filed concurrently could compromise ongoing investigations, and potentially put the public at risk.\nPaquin argues that disclosure would harm a newly-announced coroner's inquest, because \"while this is not a criminal trial, it is a court proceeding involving a jury.\" He argues that while the province had not yet called a public inquiry, any potential future inquiry could be influenced by information that had been made public.\n\"This is a large and complex investigation that has garnered significant national and international attention with significant media coverage of the incident and the police investigation,\" it reads in part.\n\"For this reason, I believe that evidence outlined in this Information to Obtain if prematurely disclosed would certainly be disseminated through various news outlets that would affect and influence the current ongoing criminal investigation, the coroner's inquest, and a potential Public Inquiry.\"\nPaquin also argues that making the contents of the document public could inspire further acts of mass violence, invoking high profile incidents in the U.S.\n\"I am aware that mass casualty incidents can inspire copycat attacks. For example, the Columbine High School massacre is well known to have had this effect where it inspired other shootings in the following years,\" he writes.\n\"I believe premature disclosure of this Information to Obtain without a complete criminal investigation, and the following Coroner's Inquest or Public Inquiry where all the facts are known could put the public at risk.\"",
        "content": "Unsealed documents reveal details of RCMP's response, investigation into Tumbler Ridge shooting\nRCMP's minute-by-minute response to the shooting is made public – though critical details remain redacted\nDocuments unsealed through efforts by CBC News and a consortium of media outlets reveal new details of the RCMP's response and investigation into the February 2026 mass shooting in Tumbler Ridge, B....",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "6m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "With U.S. midterm elections looming, Democrats are clashing over how to win",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/democratic-party-election-strategies-9.7305130?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.926220+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/c10d5e4b-2284-49c6-b429-f7374e2926e6,1786569181264/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C340%2C6516%2C3665%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "With U.S. midterm elections looming, Democrats are clashing over how to win\nGruelling primary season has further entrenched rift between progressive and centrist wings of the party\nAs the U.S. heads toward the midterm elections, a gruelling primary season has further entrenched a rift between the insurgent progressive and centrist wings of the Democratic Party.\nAt stake is control of Congress this November and, beyond that, the presidency in 2028.\nThe centrists argue candidates touting moderate and pragmatic policies is the path to flipping seats from Republican control. While progressives say more uninspiring, establishment candidates will not galvanize a coalition of young people, the working class and diverse voters that could propel the party to victory.\n\"I don't even know how I could go about being sure which group has the most appeal,\" said Hans Noel, an associate professor of Government at Georgetown University and co-director of the Georgetown Democracy Initiative.\nHe says that while progressive candidates may alienate some voters who otherwise might have voted Democrat, they will also bring new voters to the party, including those who may not have voted at all.\nThere are also intangible characteristics, like personal likeability, said Noel: \"How do you add that all up and make a strong prediction? It's hard.\"\nPrimary wins and losses\nThe primary season — where Democratic voters choose their candidates for upcoming general elections against Republicans — has given both sides wins, losses and lessons.\nOn Tuesday, in the key battleground state of Wisconsin progressives were dealt a blow when democratic voters narrowly chose moderate David Crowley in the primary for governor over self-described Democratic Socialist Francesca Hong, who was projected to win in the polls.\nHong's campaign was plagued in its final days by past comments she made advocating for defunding the police and abolishing ICE and also saying it was time to \"cancel Thanksgiving,\" calling it a celebration of colonialism. During the campaign, she backed away from all these comments, saying her views had evolved.\nHong ultimately wasn't endorsed by progressive flagbearers, Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez and Sen. Bernie Sanders.\nOther races where moderates were able to hold progressive challengers at bay included those in Washington and Missouri.\nIn the swing state of Michigan earlier this month, however, progressive Addul El-Sayed, who campaigned with Ocasio-Cortez and Sanders, eked out a victory against centrist candidate Haley Stevens despite over $60 million being spent on her behalf, largely from super PACs.\nEl-Sayed, like other progressive candidates, campaigned on issues like medicare for all, getting money out of politics, taxing billionaire wealth and calling for an end to military aid for Israel. He has also been praised for his communication skills.\nIn response to the U.S. President Donald Trump calling him \"full of shit,\" El-Sayed shot back: \"At least I don't let mine go all over the Oval Office.\"\n- Abdul El-Sayed wins Michigan's Democratic Senate primary in major victory for party's progressive wing\n- Michigan's Democratic Senate primary puts spotlight on big money and pro-Israel lobby\nEl-Sayed's victory followed wins from progressive candidates who took on more moderate democrats in strongholds of New York and Colorado.\nNotably, in Maine, another battleground state, progressive Graham Platner delivered a win before he dropped out due to sexual assault allegations.\nDiscontent among young voters\nBhaskar Sunkara, president of left-leaning magazine The Nation and former vice chair of the Democratic Socialists of America, says El-Sayed's win and others like it are a \"huge check\" on the Democratic Party.\n\"Voters, particularly young voters, are really discontent with the leadership of the party … and just generally feel that the Democratic Party doesn't stand for anything. It's not combative enough.\"\nSunkara went on to say that El-Sayed is a better candidate than Hong with working-class voters.\n\"I think there is a way in which some candidates have been tarred with stances they had in 2019, 2020. This sort of abolish the police, the discussion of whiteness or whatever else. Abdul never had that problem.\"\nHong wasn't the only candidate backing off from past comments.\nNew York congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez made headlines earlier this week when asked about her past support for defunding the police. \"I have a local city councilman that has this saying: 'Woke 1 was crazy,' \" she said before laughing.\nCan progressives win key battleground states?\nWhile some in the progressive wing re-evaluate past positions that could alienate voters this election cycle, many moderate democrats continue to warn of liabilities in swing states.\nFareed Zakaria, host of CNN's Sunday public affairs program GPS, cited Washington Post data to point out that while El-Sayed had the support of Michigan's most educated counties and college towns, his opponent did better in rural and lower-income areas.\n- AnalysisWhat the Graham Platner scandal says about Democratic Party's troubles\n- More Americans than ever are identifying as political independents, poll shows\n\"The left has the passion, but the middle still has the votes needed to win in general elections,\" Zakaria said.\nKate de Gruyter is the vice-president of communications at Third Way, a leading centrist democratic group that is spending $15 million between now and 2028 to discredit Democratic Socialism.\n\"Their theory of the case is that they're somehow going to be able to persuade all these voters in the middle that they absolutely need to win in November to back their candidate. We've never seen that happen over the entirety of the Trump era,\" she said in an interview.\nDe Gruyter added that El-Sayed's decision to campaign with controversial left-wing streamer Hasan Piker as one example of \"a whole universe of things that Republican ad makers are salivating at.\"\nDe Gruyter went on to suggest that the reason Democrat Kamala Harris lost the presidential election in 2024 was that she was seen as too lax on issues like immigration and plagued by stances she'd taken in the past on \"transgender policy.\"\n- Democrats' autopsy of 2024 U.S. election blames Harris and 'identity politics' for loss\n- Kamala Harris says she 'might' make another presidential run in 2028\nIn the final weeks of the campaign. Trump's team ran an ad using a five-year old clip of Harris saying that \"every transgender inmate in the prison system would have access\" to taxpayer-funded gender transitions.\n\"The idea that we should move further left makes no sense at all,\" she said.\nThe interesting thing is that while that can all be true, there are also plenty of arguments that suggest the opposite, according to Noel, the Georgetown associate professor.\n\"And it can also be true that Harris actually lost because she was too tied to the establishment and Biden. And too close to Israel … progressives called her a cop. Either of those differences could have been enough because it was a fairly close race.\"\nEither way, Democrats are riding favourable waves into the midterms.\nTrump's polling is low. He has entered into an unpopular war in Iran. And all of this should help the Democrats, whether they're progressives or moderates in the mid-terms, said Noel, adding that if the Democrats don't do as well as expected there will be a lot of \"what happened?\"\n\"If all the progressive candidates lose and the centrist candidates win, well, that's going to be an easier story.\"",
        "content": "With U.S. midterm elections looming, Democrats are clashing over how to win\nGruelling primary season has further entrenched rift between progressive and centrist wings of the party\nAs the U.S. heads toward the midterm elections, a gruelling primary season has further entrenched a rift between the insurgent progressive and centrist wings of the Democratic Party.\nAt stake is control of Congress this...",
