    {
        "title": "Canadian pediatricians document steep rise in severe e-scooter injuries, deaths in children, teens",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/health/e-scooters-pediatrician-survey-9.7305210?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.926213+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/5ad54906-4625-405a-8ec8-d0fe01249941,1786624788529/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C585%2C5622%2C3162%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "'Exponential rise': Canadian pediatricians document severe e-scooter injuries, deaths in children, teens\nDevices are marketed as toys, ER doctor says, despite quick acceleration to high speeds\nAs e-scooters become increasingly common across Canada, pediatricians, emergency physicians and other experts warn that injuries — often severe and involving younger people — are rising, while regulations struggle to keep pace.\nDr. Daniel Rosenfield, a pediatric emergency physician at SickKids hospital in Toronto and chair of the injury prevention committee for the Canadian Pediatric Society (CPS), says he has seen \"a complete exponential rise\" in every type of injury that can result from an e-scooter crash — from minor scrapes to major brain injuries and death.\nBad injuries used to be \"once in a blue moon,\" he told CBC News. Now, \"anytime we get a call from EMS that says, 'E-scooter trauma inbound,' we're prepared for severe injuries,\" he said.\nE-scooter injury cases at SickKids rose from 26 in 2023 to 46 in 2024 and 107 in 2025, Rosenfield said.\n\"And this year we're likely on target for more than that.\"\nAt the Montreal Children’s Hospital, e-scooter injuries have increased by a factor of 10 since 2023, said Dr. Laurie Plotnick, who heads the hospital’s emergency trauma team.\nChildren, who were injured without the involvement of another motor vehicle, have suffered \"severe facial injuries … broken jaws, broken bones around the eyes,\" she said.\n\"We're talking about severe brain injuries.\"\nThe experiences at those hospitals are mirrored across Canada and around the world. A CPS survey of Canadian pediatric specialists, conducted from late 2025 to early 2026 and published Thursday, documented 10 deaths, 12 ICU admissions and 26 hospitalizations lasting more than two days over the previous 12 months as a result of e-scooter crashes. Head injuries were present in about half the cases.\n\"The vast majority, if not all, are unhelmeted,\" Rosenfield said.\nThe most severe injuries tended to involve boys between 10 and 15 years old, he said.\n- E-scooter rider taken to out-of-region hospital after being hit by an SUV in Waterloo\n- Driver hits 12-year-old on a scooter, flees scene in St. John’s, says RNC\nThe CPS survey records the experience of pediatric specialists rather than providing a rigorous quantitative analysis. But a separate study published Tuesday in the Emergency Medicine Journal provides multi-year hospital administrative data from three U.K. hospitals that closely aligns with what Canadian pediatricians are seeing.\nThat study found e-scooter cases at hospitals in Liverpool, Manchester and Sheffield rose from fewer than 10 in 2019 to almost 150 in 2024. Head injuries were among the most severe, and fewer than two per cent of those injured were wearing helmets.\nFrom 69 km/h to a critical care unit\nThat U.K. data mirrors what Canadian families are experiencing firsthand.\nJust over a year ago in London, Ont., Crystal Dunkley's son Shaun was not wearing a helmet when he took a family friend's e-scooter out for a ride one evening, as he had done many times before.\nThat evening, Shaun, then 12, hit a raccoon as it ran across his path.\n\"He believes he was going 69 kilometres an hour when this happened,\" Crystal Dunkley recalled in a video interview Wednesday.\nAn ambulance rushed Shaun to the Children's Hospital in London, where he was intubated and admitted to the Pediatric Critical Care Unit with a fractured skull. He was in a coma for over a week.\n\"The doctors told us that with the brain bleed they determined he had, there was a possibility he would not make it, and if he did, then he would likely be very disabled,\" Crystal said.\nShaun ultimately recovered completely after 34 days in Children's Hospital and more time at a rehabilitation facility in Toronto. Crystal said he now approaches people riding e-scooters on the street and tells them to wear a helmet.\n\"Not a day goes by that I don't see multiple young children riding them without helmets, sometimes two or three to a scooter,\" Crystal said. \"Some of these scooters can go … like I said, the one he was on was going 69 kilometres an hour.\"\n'Anything but toys': Why regulation is lagging behind\nMost parents are surprised by the extent of their child's injuries, Rosenfield said.\n\"And to be fair to the families, these devices are often marketed as toys,\" he said. \"And as a device that can go zero to 40 kilometres an hour in a number of seconds, I will tell you they're anything but toys.\"\nThe CPS survey also notes that younger people may be less familiar with road rules, less risk-averse and at greater risk of injury because of their developmental stage.\nBut it also argues there are problems with rules and laws, describing them as \"unclear and made up of a patchwork of regulations, varying depending on the province/territory.\"\nMany parents simply aren’t aware of the rules in their province or city, said Plotnick, the Montreal Children’s Hospital physician. “Parents are surprised when they have children who are under 14 severely injured … to hear that actually the legal age is 14 in Quebec.”\nThe minimum age for riding an e-scooter varies by province, while some cities have banned the devices altogether — though those bans are often ignored. Several provinces, including B.C., Ontario and Quebec, cap e-scooter speeds at 24 or 25 kilometres per hour.\n\"I can tell you for sure people go faster than that,\" Rosenfield said.\n“We need public education,” said Plotnick. “We need to educate parents. We need to lobby for manufacturers to have clear messaging on the products and we need better enforcement.”\nRosenfield is not advocating for an e-scooter ban, pointing to their utility to complement public transit and reduce automobile traffic.\n\"I do think they have a use in an urban environment,\" he said. \"I just think we need to figure out what that looks like and how to make those devices safe.\"\nWith files from Melanie Glanz",
        "content": "'Exponential rise': Canadian pediatricians document severe e-scooter injuries, deaths in children, teens\nDevices are marketed as toys, ER doctor says, despite quick acceleration to high speeds\nAs e-scooters become increasingly common across Canada, pediatricians, emergency physicians and other experts warn that injuries — often severe and involving younger people — are rising, while regulations st...",
