        "title": "Man was partially sucked out of plane after broken engine blade shattered window, investigators find",
        "link": "https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/ryanair-flight-plane-window-shattered-man-sucked-out-9.7306319?cmp=rss",
        "source": "CBC | Top Stories News",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.926190+00:00",
        "image": "https://i.cbc.ca/ais/4146b417-1ba5-4ee1-acb4-31c445c70ab1,1786648330527/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C260%2C5000%2C2812%29%3BResize%3D620",
        "full_content": "Broken engine part shattered Ryanair plane window that partially sucked out passenger: investigators\nU.S. National Transportation Safety Board says it found bird remains inside engine\nBroken engine parts shattered a window on a Ryanair plane shortly after takeoff in Greece last month, causing a man's head to be sucked into the hole in the fuselage before his fellow passengers pulled him to safety, U.S. investigators said Thursday.\nParts that flew out of the engine after a fan blade broke damaged a window and the fuselage in several other places, the U.S. National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) said in its report.\nThe incident happened shortly after the flight took off from the northern Greek city of Thessaloniki on July 10. The NTSB said it found bird remains inside the engine, suggesting the plane may have struck birds as it took off.\nThe 61-year-old man who was nearly sucked out of the plane suffered neck and shoulder injuries and friction burns before his fellow passengers pulled him back inside the Boeing 737-800.\nThe flight attendants told investigators they heard and felt loud, continuous vibration and saw smoke or fog inside the cabin before oxygen masks dropped from the ceiling.\nOne flight attendant then noticed passengers standing and calling for help because man sitting in a window seat in row 11 was partially lodged in a damaged window. Other passengers managed to pull him back into the cabin.\nThe injured passenger was moved to row 12, where a passenger who was a doctor tended to him. Other passengers got a metal box from the flight attendants and used it to try to block the broken window until the plane could land.\nA series of short videos recorded from inside the plane and shared by Radio Thessaloniki showed passengers wearing oxygen masks after the cabin lost pressure. Another appeared to show the blown-out window, with a man seated nearby wearing an oxygen mask. A third video, apparently filmed after the aircraft landed, showed first responders working in the aisle.\nLast month, Ryanair said in a statement that the flight \"returned to Thessaloniki shortly after takeoff when a passenger window dislodged in-flight.\"\nInternational aviation rules allow Greece's Hellenic Air and Rail Safety Investigation Authority to hand over investigations to the NTSB, which they did in this case, with Greece participating.\nThe NTSB reprimanded Ryanair CEO Michael O'Leary after he told investors last month that the investigation was focused on foreign object damage and not the plane's age or maintenance history. The safety board said investigators hadn't ruled anything out and O'Leary wasn't authorized to discuss it.\nFan blades had undergone inspections\nMaintenance records show the fan blades on the right engine that failed underwent ultrasonic inspections in May 2026 and November 2025, with no damage found. But the NTSB said that four suspected bird strikes to the same engine had been reported by flight crews over the past year. Bird remains were found after two of those suspected strikes.\nThe Associated Press sent emailed requests for comment on NTSB's findings to Ryanair, Boeing and CFM International, the maker of the engine.\nThere have been at least two previous incidents where parts of an engine made by CFM failed and breached the plane's cabin. In 2018, a woman was killed on a Southwest Airlines flight after she was partially sucked out of a broken window. Another Southwest jet suffered a similar blade-related engine failure over Florida in 2016.\nThe narrow-body plane, which can seat up to 189 passengers, was delivered new to Ryanair in 2008, according to flight-tracking site Flightradar24.\nFlight records show the aircraft climbed past 4,570 metres about six minutes after departure and then immediately descended to about 1,830 metres \"to burn fuel for 30 minutes\" before returning to Thessaloniki about an hour after taking off, Flightradar24 said.\nThe flight was operated by Malta Air, a subsidiary of Ryanair, Europe's largest budget carrier.\nThe plane landed normally and passengers returned to the terminal, and one passenger requested and received medical assistance on the ground in Thessaloniki, the airline said in a statement at the time.",
        "content": "Broken engine part shattered Ryanair plane window that partially sucked out passenger: investigators\nU.S. National Transportation Safety Board says it found bird remains inside engine\nBroken engine parts shattered a window on a Ryanair plane shortly after takeoff in Greece last month, causing a man's head to be sucked into the hole in the fuselage before his fellow passengers pulled him to safety,...",
