        "title": "Someone in Illinois Just Won $1.04 Billion in the Powerball—Legally, They Can Stay Anonymous Forever",
        "link": "https://thoughtcatalog.com/january-nelson/2026/08/someone-in-illinois-just-won-1-04-billion-in-the-powerball-legally-they-can-stay-anonymous-forever/",
        "source": "Thought Catalog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.925454+00:00",
        "image": "https://thoughtcatalog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/someone-in-illinois-just-won-1-04-billion-in-the-powerball-legally-they-can-stay.jpg?w=1536&h=768&crop=1",
        "full_content": "Someone in Illinois Just Won $1.04 Billion in the Powerball—Legally, They Can Stay Anonymous Forever\nA single ticket bought at a gas station in Quincy, Illinois just made somebody into a billionaire.\nThe Powerball jackpot has been rolling over since May and Wednesday’s drawing was for $1.04 billion. The winning ticket was sold at a Hy-Vee gas station on 36th Street in Quincy, Illinois, according to state lottery officials.\nThe winner has not yet come forward.\nThe winner can take the estimated $1.040 billion annuity, paid in 30 installments over 29 years, or a $450.5 million lump-sum cash payout before taxes. Most winners take the cash.\nThe winning numbers were 4-26-66-67-69 with a Powerball of 9.\nNobody had hit the jackpot since May 2, when tickets in Texas and Florida split a $20 million prize. Drawing after drawing rolled over from there, building into one of the largest jackpots Powerball has ever awarded.\nThere’s a good chance the public never learns who won. Illinois lets winners of $250,000 or more keep their name and city confidential by checking a box when they claim, and big-jackpot winners routinely take it. To collect, the ticket holder signs the back and brings it and ID to lottery headquarters (usually by appointment for a prize this size). They have up to one year to claim.\nThen the tax bite. Federal withholding starts at 24%, but the real federal rate runs up to 37%, and Illinois takes its cut on top. A cash winner typically walks away with roughly half of the $450.5 million headline number.\nThat’s why winners usually go quiet for days or weeks first. Lawyers, financial advisors, trusts for privacy and tax planning, and a plan for the flood of calls from family, friends, and strangers all get lined up before anyone signs anything.\nThe night had other winners too. A player in Massachusetts won $2 million, and four more took home $1 million each on tickets sold in Arizona, California, Florida, and North Carolina.\nThe next drawing is Saturday, Aug. 15, and the jackpot resets to an estimated $20 million.\nLike what you read? Follow Thought Catalog on Facebook, and check out more on our website.",
