    {
        "title": "After this week's Elder Scrolls 6 tease, fans think they've figured out its full name - and it was hidden in Starfield all along",
        "link": "https://www.eurogamer.net/the-elder-scrolls-6-subtitle-fan-theory-starfield",
        "source": "Eurogamer.net Latest Articles Feed",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.925049+00:00",
        "image": "https://assetsio.gnwcdn.com/TGS6.jpg?width=1200&height=630&fit=crop&enable=upscale&auto=webp",
        "full_content": "After this week's Elder Scrolls 6 tease, fans think they've figured out its full name - and it was hidden in Starfield all along\nWatch this space.\nFollowing Xbox CEO Asha Sharma's recent redacted tease of a subtitle for the long-awaited Elder Scrolls 6, it's been discovered the game's full name might have been hiding in developer Bethesda's most recent release, Starfield, all along.\nEarlier this week, Sharma - whose recent visit to developer Bethesda was met with a giant inflatable rat outside its offices as protests against Xbox's devastating layoffs continue - teased that she'd just had a \"live playthrough of The Elder Scrolls 6: ******** this morning.\" The eight asterisks in her post naturally inspired players to go digging for answers, sharing theories as to what the game's subtitle could be.\nSome of the suggestions included Dominion, Covenant, Orsinium, Starfall and even Skyrim 2 - which has eight characters if you count the space. However, the leading theory so far appears to be Sentinel, a major city in The Elder Scrolls' region of Hammerfell. Even before Sharma's tweet, many theorised Hammerfell is where the next game would be set, based mostly on the single image Bethesda shared when The Elder Scrolls 6 was announced in 2018.\nBut it's now emerged that Bethesda may have been hiding the game's name - which just so happens to be the community's current favourite subtitle candidate - in Starfield for years (via IGN). Or, at least, that's the latest theory.\nOn Reddit, players have been revisiting screenshots of the Starfield character creator, which includes a 16-digit employee number at the bottom. This is typically the type of incidental detail that lends authenticity to fictional worlds, but in this case, there's compelling evidence it's also been secretly spelling out 'Sentinel' this entire time. When the number is divided into pairs, then each is mapped to a corresponding letter in the alphabet, you get the magic word: Sentinel.\nBy the way, Sentinel was already teased in Starfield\nby u/starfieldnovember in TESVI\nThis isn't a new discovery, mind: some players picked up on it three years ago, and even posted about it on Reddit. But the discovery has now resurfaced as potential evidence that 'Sentinel' is indeed the Elder Scrolls 6's subtitle. What's more, former Bethesda writer, artist, and designer Michael Kirkbride has chimed in to confirm the Sentinel theory, which is probably about the best we're going to get at this point.\nIn 2025, Bethesda's Todd Howard revealed The Elder Scrolls 6 was \"still a long way off\" from release. And last month, in the wake of Xbox's sweeping layoffs, Bethesda employees reportedly claimed the cuts would have a \"substantial and cascading effect\" on the game's development progress.\nBethesda later released a statement intended to assuage concerned fans, claiming the studio's current primary focus is on delivering the long-awaited sequel, though it did not address the reported employee claims directly. \"The next chapter is on the way,\" the studio said in its message. \"We’re where we planned to be, loving how it looks, and playing it every day.\"\nEight years after its unveiling, The Elder Scrolls 6 still doesn't have a release target.\nPC, PS5, Xbox Series X/S\nSee 1 more\nPC, Xbox Series X/S",
        "content": "After this week's Elder Scrolls 6 tease, fans think they've figured out its full name - and it was hidden in Starfield all along\nWatch this space.\nFollowing Xbox CEO Asha Sharma's recent redacted tease of a subtitle for the long-awaited Elder Scrolls 6, it's been discovered the game's full name might have been hiding in developer Bethesda's most recent release, Starfield, all along.\nEarlier this w...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "6m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "20 hours in, I cannot believe the scale, complexity, and ambition of Fire Emblem: Fortune's Weave",
        "link": "https://www.eurogamer.net/fire-emblem-fortunes-weave-preview",
        "source": "Eurogamer.net Latest Articles Feed",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.925046+00:00",
        "image": "https://assetsio.gnwcdn.com/Fire-Emblem-header-1.jpg?width=1200&height=630&fit=crop&enable=upscale&auto=webp",
        "full_content": "20 hours in, I cannot believe the scale, complexity, and ambition of Fire Emblem: Fortune's Weave\nIntelligent System upgrade.\nFire Emblem: Fortune's Weave is massive. If you're coming to Intelligent Systems' Switch 2 RPG after playing even Three Houses or Engage, you're going to be shocked by the scale of it. Within the first 10 hours of the game, you are given choices that show you just how broad the scope of the game is. As you play, more avenues open up before you in concertina, a bafflingly complex bloom of consequence, intrigue, and narrative that blossoms and blossoms.\nI'm almost intimidated by it. From the trailers, you've probably seen that there are multiple playable characters whose stories you can follow; the four main protagonists are Theodora (a warrior queen determined to do right by her people), Dietrech (a foreign swordsman intrigued by this land's martial customs), Cai (a young village boy caught up in a peculiar conspiracy), and Leda (a traveling performer lusting for revenge). How Intelligent Systems has crammed this into a 29.5GB file, I'll never know.\nWatch on YouTube\nInstantly, the game establishes one clear maxim: this is not Engage. There is not one path. This is a labyrinthine, deliciously textured story you're going to need a wall chart and plenty of string in order to keep up with. It makes the plot of Three Houses look indolent by comparison. I'm only allowed to talk about the first few chapters in this preview, but that's enough. You only need to play for a few hours to see the potential this game has. It looks like it's going to be the biggest Fire Emblem ever - and possibly the most definitive.\nAfter an introductory sequence that lands some pretty heavy contextualisation regarding the fate of the world, you start to unpick the knotty story about the four heroes' journey through the Heroic Games: a tournament hosted in the region's capital city of Dagison that will grant one of the protagonists an audience with the Divine Sovereign, who will in turn grant them one wish. Unity, vengeance, knowledge, power - it's all very fantasy, with enough Proper Nouns thrown into the mix to make George R. R. Martin blush. There is so much lore in the first 30 minutes alone that I'm sure Fire Emblem historians are going to have a field day, unearthing all the links to past games and in-universe events. But even if you're not a lore goblin, you're going to find the way that Intelligent Systems has just grown everything outwards is stunning.\nLet's start with combat, Fire Emblem's beating heart. The turn-based, grid-restricted system is back and - on the surface - the same as it ever was. Choose a unit, move them around, attack an enemy, see how they respond. Rinse, repeat. But certain units - like chariots - have special skills beyond simple attacks that allow them to, say, mow down entire rows of enemies. More advanced classes can combine offensive weapons (like spears) with utility (like white magic). Each protagonist has access to special abilities that make them stand out on the battlefield and have more functionality than their wards and guards - I played as Theodara initially, and her penchant for chucking rocks across the battlefield ended some battles before they even began in earnest. But these abilities cost HP, and more often than not if your lead unit dies, you lose. It's risk/reward writ large.\nIntelligent Systems is throwing off the training weights with Fortune's Weave, I think. There has always been plenty of choice in the Fire Emblem series, but here, it feels like we get access to a full toybox far earlier on than in Three Houses, Fates, or Awakening. You can start recruiting other characters to your army nice and early, too, giving you a distinct sense of achievement and intent from the little hours onwards. Whilst many units can use multiple weapons, the way that specialist classes have been tinkered with means you can build ultra-specific strategies on the battlefield - more so than in any Fire Emblem game I've played to date. Enemy AI (at least on the harder modes) seems cannier and crueller, too: I found them routinely targeting my weakest unit and rolling the dice with their critical shots, to sometimes disgusting effect, even in the earlier missions. Mascochists, they finally listened to us!\nThe animation and attack systems have been overhauled, too: with so many new units - and a couple of special weapons - we need more texture. The skirmishes themselves look remarkable; dynamic, responsive, punchy. The only criticism I'll level here comes down to critical hits; they land quite lethargically, perhaps undercut by the flashy animations that precede them. Back in the Path of Radiance days, crits used to land like the splash of a cymbal; here they're more like a dampened snare. Not bad, just different. And a tiny nitpick in the scheme of things, really.\nBetween your qualifiers and elimination rounds in the Heroic Games, you're given free time (Persona-style) that's measured in turns. Here you'll encounter a frankly embarrassing number of NPCs that can be charmed, recruited, or trained to beef you up for the coming fights. Exploring the capital city actually takes more time than battling, in my experience so far, because there is so much to do. Train at the arena, go perform tasks for the Gods, engage with side missions, run errands for townsfolk, comb the world for supplies to send back to your homeland, upgrade your weapons, trade with multiple different trading companies that even have their own stories, perform in plays, go to the spa, eat a meal at the tavern, find random encounters out on the world map (inspired by medieval Spain)… the list goes on, and on, and on.\nEach of the four protagonists have their 'main characters' for their respective parties, but that doesn't mean you can't chat to - and try to recruit - loyal subjects from other teams. I've played as Theodora and Cai so far, and I am stunned by the amount of character-specific dialogue in this game. It reminds me of playing a PS1 RPG with how much volume there is, how reactive the world is to who you are, the possibilities that feel like they're running out in front of you at any given moment. There are also bond links between characters - now a Fire Emblem staple - that give you even more (voiced!) dialogue if you have two people who spend enough time together.\nAnd, of course, all this socialising makes you stronger. Once you've got a tight knit army full of companionable allies, you can take to the world map: a large-scale overworld view that takes time to traverse, meaning you have to plan your forays in advance. But, out here in the wild, there's yet another aspect of Fire Emblem: Fortune's Weave that is really surprising: dungeons. These make a return from the 3DS title, Fire Emblem Echoes: Shadows of Valentia, and include their own dedicated battle system that removes the grid-based SRPG stuff entirely and replaces it with a turn-based system more comparable to old-school Final Fantasy or Dragon Quest. They're not particularly deep or revelatory, but in a game that is probably going to take hundreds of hours to complete, they're a nice change of pace and provide a decent way of levelling up combatants you may neglect in the more plot-critical encounters.\nThere is so much more to go into, but I don't want to spoil too much of the game for people that want to go in somewhat blind. Safe to say, though, that even in the opening hours of Fire Emblem: Fortune's Weave, I feel like this is one of the best games in the series. It's already, by far, the best and most ambitious game I've played on the Switch 2. Who knows how the back half will feel (and I actually think this is where both Three Houses and Engage lose marks for me; they become top-heavy and fail to 'stick the landing', so to speak). But from my time with Fortune's Weave so far, I am compelled to say: I don't think Fire Emblem has ever looked, or felt, better.\nFire Emblem: Fortune's Weave arrives 17th September, 2026, exclusively on Switch 2.\nRead this next\n- Fire Emblem: Fortune's Weave's world map is partially inspired by medieval Spain, even if the Dagdan Empire setting looks much older\n- Fire Emblem: Fortune's Weave crams itself into an already packed-out September 2026 on Switch 2\n- Nintendo Direct live report - all the news and announcements from the summer 2026 show",
        "content": "20 hours in, I cannot believe the scale, complexity, and ambition of Fire Emblem: Fortune's Weave\nIntelligent System upgrade.\nFire Emblem: Fortune's Weave is massive. If you're coming to Intelligent Systems' Switch 2 RPG after playing even Three Houses or Engage, you're going to be shocked by the scale of it. Within the first 10 hours of the game, you are given choices that show you just how broad...",
