        "title": "Manage bare metal like a cloud with Red Hat Bare-Metal-as-a-Service for OpenShift",
        "link": "https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/manage-bare-metal-cloud-red-hat-bare-metal-service-openshift",
        "source": "Red Hat Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:06:17.923864+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.redhat.com/themes/custom/rhdc/img/red-hat-social-share.jpg",
        "full_content": "Red Hat OpenShift 4.22 includes general availability support for Red Hat Bare-Metal-as-a-Service (BMaas) for OpenShift, enabling organizations to manage bare metal, virtual machines (VMs), and application containers using the same consistent platform.\nWhat is Red Hat Bare-Metal-as-a-Service for OpenShift?\nBefore OpenShift 4.22, Cluster Baremetal Operator in OpenShift provided centralized management for servers with Redfish support with containerized and virtualized workloads. With this new add-on, Red Hat OpenShift Container Platform now extends its orchestration capabilities to physical hardware running an operating system (OS) only, outside of the traditional virtualization layer. This allows you to manage a fleet of servers with the same efficiency you expect when managing hardware with VMs or containers on top of OpenShift. The CBO is produced by Metal3, an incubating project within the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) that provides Kubernetes-native bare metal host management, enrollment, OS provisioning, and lifecycle management of physical servers.\nWhy bare metal for AI and HPC?\nWhile virtualization offers flexibility, certain high-performance workloads require direct access to physical hardware to eliminate virtualization overhead. This is particularly critical for:\n- Artificial intelligence and machine learning (AI/ML): Managing a fleet of servers with GPU cards to maximize processing power.\n- High-performance computing (HPC): Running intensive computational tasks on a specific OS without performance penalties.\nUsing BMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift, you can deploy an OpenShift cluster that manages servers with an OS-only configuration specifically for these high-demand use cases.\nKey capabilities of BMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift\nBMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift is designed to meet several business requirements for modern infrastructure:\n- Unified application platform: Manage containers, VMs, and bare metal instances from one location.\n- Firmware management: Configure and upgrade firmware, including BIOS and BMC.\n- Multi-OS provisioning: Provision bare metal hosts with Red Hat Enterprise Linux (it requires a Red Hat Enterprise Linux SKU), Microsoft Windows, or other operating systems. Note the customer is responsible for providing the OS image.\n- Rapid provisioning: Benefit from dynamic provisioning and deprovisioning that mirrors the cloud experience.\n- Network configuration: Provision a boot instance.\n- Image management: Store, manage, and retrieve various OS images through a centralized repository provided by the customer.\nExtending management with Red Hat Ansible Automation Platform\nWhile BMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift handles the initial provisioning, firmware configuration, and hardware lifecycle, you can enhance your environment with Red Hat Ansible Automation Platform. Ansible Automation Platform provides optional lifecycle management for the OS and external components like top-of-rack (ToR) switches and routers.\nPlease note that Red Hat Ansible Automation Platform requires a dedicated SKU and is not included in the Red Hat Bare-Metal-as-a-Service for OpenShift SKU.\nArchitecture overview: BMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift\nFigure 1 illustrates how the BMaaS for Red Hat Openshift functionality interacts with your hardware and external resources to facilitate automated management. We have noted in the figure the specific OSes that we have tested, but the other listed OSes should work as well; we are not providing an OS image as part of this feature.\nFigure 1. High-level BMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift architectural diagram showing centralized management of OS-based workloads via the Cluster Baremetal Operator.\nSupport and availability\nThis new functionality is a part of Red Hat OpenShift. If you would like to use it, you need a dedicated subscription (SKU) for bare metal nodes with OS only (OpenShift control plane nodes do not require a SKU). To keep your critical workloads operational, Red Hat offers both Standard and Premium support levels for BMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift.\nWe encourage customers and partners to explore how BMaaS for Red Hat OpenShift can simplify their infrastructure. If you have questions about technical requirements or subscriptions, please contact your Red Hat account team for more information. You can also learn more from the Red OpenShift Container Platform documentation.\nProduct trial\nRed Hat OpenShift Virtualization Engine | Product Trial\nAbout the authors\nMore like this\nWhat is metal to agents? Navigating the architecture of enterprise AI\nOperationalizing agentic AI: The Day 0-2 blueprint for enterprise infrastructure\nCan Compliance Be A Piece Of Cake? | Compiler\nStandardizing the AI stack with PyTorch\nBrowse by channel\nAutomation\nThe latest on IT automation for tech, teams, and environments\nArtificial intelligence\nUpdates on the platforms that free customers to run AI workloads anywhere\nOpen hybrid cloud\nExplore how we build a more flexible future with hybrid cloud\nSecurity\nThe latest on how we reduce risks across environments and technologies\nEdge computing\nUpdates on the platforms that simplify operations at the edge\nInfrastructure\nThe latest on the world’s leading enterprise Linux platform\nApplications\nInside our solutions to the toughest application challenges\nVirtualization\nThe future of enterprise virtualization for your workloads on-premise or across clouds",
