        "title": "How to compare two Word documents",
        "link": "https://zapier.com/blog/compare-two-word-documents",
        "source": "The Zapier Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.362532+00:00",
        "image": "https://images.ctfassets.net/lzny33ho1g45/create-pdf-p-img/842c18825eccb2cda4a22e2da171a20f/resume-hero.jpg",
        "full_content": "I used to compare two documents by positioning them side by side on one small screen and then manually scanning for errors, sentence by sentence. As you'd expect, that method morphed my face into an alarming scrunch, and more importantly, it was ripe for human error.\nThen I learned that every word processing app I use has a built-in tool to do exactly this.\nIf you're straining your eyes to spot the difference between two documents, here's how to compare two Word documents in the desktop app. Note: There's no built-in tool to compare documents in the web version of Word.\nTable of contents:\nHow to compare two Word documents\nMicrosoft makes it really easy to compare two Word documents in the desktop app, but it isn't immediately obvious.\n- Open a new document in Word.\n- In the upper ribbon, click Review.\n- Click Compare, and then click Compare Documents.\n- In the Compare Documents window that appears, update the following fields: \n  - Original document: Choose the first document you want to use in your comparison.\n  - Revised document: Choose the other version of the document that you want to use in your comparison.\n  - Label changes with: To make it easier to spot differences, assign a label—for example, the writer's name—to mark changes in the revised document.\n  - In: By default, Word will display changes between the two documents in the original document. I prefer to compare documents in a new document in case I ever want to revert back to the original of either version. To do this, click New document in the Show Changes section. (If this option isn't visible, click the down caret [∨].)\n- Click OK.\nWord then merges the two documents together and tracks differences as comments in the Markup Area in the side panel of the doc. If you're scanning through just the doc, Word also applies a red font color and underlines any differences in text.\nHow to merge Word documents\nLet's say you want to incorporate all the changes from the second version into the original document, without the markups. Here's how to quickly do this after you've compared both documents.\n- Click the down caret ( ∨ ) next to Accept in the Review tab.\n- Click Accept All Changes. Or, you can click Accept and Move to Next to manually review each change and merge select edits.\nIf you compare two documents in a new document, Word takes changes from the Revised document (the second file) and merges them into the Original document.\nAnd if you want to merge Word documents without comparing them first, here's how.\n- Click Review in the upper ribbon.\n- Click Compare, and then click Combine Documents.\n- In the Combine Documents window that appears, select the two documents you want to combine.\n- Click OK.\nWhen you're done, don't forget to hit the Ctrl+S shortcut to save your changes.\nCompare vs. combine in Word: What's the difference?\nWhere compare lets you easily see the differences between two documents, combine lets you merge two documents with different changes made by multiple editors into a single document. Use combine when you already have edits (tracked changes) in the drafts.\nNeither of these features is available on Word for Web because the online version tracks changes similarly to Google Docs.\nHow to compare two Word documents side by side\nIf you prefer to do visual gymnastics, you can also compare two Word documents side by side.\n- Open the two Word documents you want to compare.\n- In either of the documents, click View.\n- In the Window group, click View Side by Side. (You might only see the icon for it, which looks like two documents next to each other.)\n- Optionally, click Synchronous Scrolling to scroll both documents at the same time.\nOnce you're done comparing documents, click View Side by Side again to return to your normal, single file view.\nHow to compare documents in Google Docs\nThe steps to compare two documents in Google Docs varies slightly from Word, but it's just as straightforward to do.\n- Open the first document you want to compare.\n- In the menu, click Tools, and then click Compare documents.\n- In the Compare documents window that appears, select the second document you want to use in your comparison, and enter a name in the Attribute differences to field. You can even include comments from the second document. Then click Compare.\n- In the Comparison is ready window that appears, click Open. This will create a new document. (Unlike Word, there's no option to populate changes into the original document.)\nSimilar to Word, any differences between the two documents are tracked in suggestion mode as Google Docs comments. And to give you an at-a-glance view of differences directly in the doc, Google Docs treats changes with a different font color.\nHow to merge documents in Google Docs\nGoogle Docs doesn't have a dedicated tool to combine two documents. But there is a workaround.\n- Follow the above steps to compare documents.\n- In the new document's menu, click Tools, then click Review suggested edits.\n- To accept all the suggested edits at once, click Accept All. If you want to manually review each suggestion first, click the up (∧) or down (∨) caret next to Suggested edits, and the Accept All button will automatically change to Accept. Now you can accept edits one at a time.\nYou can also preview what your document would look like if you accepted all the edits. Click the down caret (v) next to Show suggested edits, and then select Preview \"Accept all.\" That way, you can look at the document before you end up having to manually correct weird capitalization or other formatting issues.\nHow to compare documents and files using free tools\nLet's say you want to quickly compare two versions of text that weren't originally drafted in Word or Google Docs. There are online tools designed to do just this. Some even let you compare PDFs, PowerPoint presentations, and code.\nHere are three document and file comparison tools you can use for free.\nDraftable\nDraftable is a ridiculously easy-to-use free tool that lets you compare Word documents, PowerPoint presentations (only .ppt, not .pptx), and PDFs. \nUpload your two files, click Compare, and in seconds, you'll have a side-by-side comparison with differences highlighted directly in the doc and listed to the side (similar to Word and Google Docs).\nQuickDiff\nIf you have only plain text to compare—as in, no formatting like bold and underline or elements like tables and hyperlinks—then QuickDiff is a decent option.\nCopy and paste the two bits of text you want to compare, and then click Check for differences. You can even tell QuickDiff to ignore changes like inserted or deleted blank lines and letter case.\nThere are a few downsides to QuickDiff worth mentioning.\n- Spots errors at a sentence or paragraph level only. QuickDiff highlights entire sections (sentences or paragraphs) that contain differences. This means that if there are minor changes within any given sentence or paragraph, you still have to manually review that section to find the difference.\n- Limited accessibility. QuickDiff uses only visual cues to highlight differences, which isn't helpful if you have limited vision.\nDiffchecker\nDiffchecker is a full file comparison tool that can highlight differences in code, documents, and images. It has a web app, but you can also buy the desktop version if you want to avoid uploading your documents to the platform's cloud servers.\nThe platform relies on optical character recognition (OCR) to scan PDFs, compare images, and scan multiple Excel documents. To scan full folders, you'll need to pay for the Pro version, which starts at $21/user/month.\nHere are some of the downsides of choosing Diffchecker:\n- There's no mobile support. Diffchecker doesn't have a mobile application for \"on-the-go\" document checking.\n- The desktop version requires payment. The free version requires you to upload documents or paste text individually, while the desktop version lets you access files without uploading them to a cloud server.\nPlay spot the difference for fun—not for work\nI love a good game of Spot the difference when it's strictly for entertainment (or a cheesy participation ribbon). But the stakes are infinitely higher when you need to do it in a professional capacity.\nDon't risk the chance of human error—or scaring off your coworkers if you make the same furiously scrunched face I do. Use your word processor's built-in features or an online comparison tool to spot the differences for you.\nAnd whether you're using Word's built-in compare tool, Google Docs, or a dedicated app, catching every edit is only half the battle—the other half is making sure the right version ends up in the right place.\nZapier lets you connect your favorite word processor to the rest of your stack to automatically archive finalized drafts, alert teammates when a document's ready for review, or route approved edits straight into your CMS or project management tool. Automate the process, or access and make changes to your documents straight from your AI assistant.\nRelated reading:\nThis article was originally published in August 2023. The most recent update, with contributions from Eli Smith, was in August 2026.",
        "content": "I used to compare two documents by positioning them side by side on one small screen and then manually scanning for errors, sentence by sentence. As you'd expect, that method morphed my face into an alarming scrunch, and more importantly, it was ripe for human error.\nThen I learned that every word processing app I use has a built-in tool to do exactly this.\nIf you're straining your eyes to spot th...",
