        "title": "The architecture of enterprise trust: Twilio US Branded Calling is now generally available",
        "link": "https://www.twilio.com/en-us/blog/products/launches/branded-calling-general-availability",
        "source": "Twilio Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.362415+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.twilio.com/content/dam/twilio-com/global/en/solutions/branded-calling/branded_calling_hero_twilio_v2.png",
        "full_content": "The architecture of enterprise trust: Twilio US Branded Calling is now generally available\nTime to read:\n- Branded Calling helps businesses stand out by displaying verified business names and logos on calls.\n- This feature can improve customer trust, leading to higher answer rates and successful outreach.\n- Twilio simplifies onboarding and management, integrating verification within existing platforms.\nThis summary was generated by AI and reviewed by the Twilio team.\nThe architecture of enterprise trust: Twilio US Branded Calling is now generally available\nThe death of the unbranded call: Why 86% of your outbound budget is ignored\nSpam calls have made it harder for businesses to reach customers in a meaningful way. Most people send unknown numbers to voicemail, so real conversations and important connections are missed. What used to be a simple phone call is now a gamble that leads to missed appointments, stalled sales, and wasted outreach.\nThat’s why today, we’re announcing that Branded Calling is now generally available in the US.\nIt comes as businesses face a growing challenge: customers increasingly do not trust unknown calls. Hiya’s 2026 State of the Call report found that 86% of consumers do not answer unknown phone numbers. 73% say businesses should identify themselves in caller ID, and 29% say seeing the verified business name is the top thing that would make them more likely to answer. At the same time, 34% say trust in phone calls has decreased, and 31% say they have received a deepfake voice call.\nBranded Calling helps businesses make important outbound calls more recognizable by displaying a verified business name, logo, and, where supported, reason for calling directly on supported mobile devices. This GA release also makes Branded Calling easier to onboard, manage, and scale for the teams behind the calls.\nThe outbound trust deficit is real\nThe voice channel still matters for high-value moments. But it is harder to earn attention when an incoming call looks unfamiliar.\nThat creates real operational friction for teams running appointment reminders, fraud alerts, delivery coordination, account servicing, and follow-up outreach. If a customer does not recognize the call, the workflow can stall before the conversation even starts.\nIt is not just a customer experience issue. It is also an operational one. Many teams still manage identity, onboarding, and rollout across fragmented systems, which adds extra work for operations, legal, and IT teams before a program ever goes live.\nThe solution: recognizable calls, with a faster path to launch\nTwilio Branded Calling is designed to help legitimate outbound calls look more recognizable at the moment the phone rings.\nInstead of asking teams to stitch together a separate branded-calling workflow, Twilio connects branded identity to the same platform they already use to build, launch, and manage voice communications. That means businesses can improve how calls appear to recipients while also giving internal teams a more centralized way to onboard and operate the experience.\nHow it works\nBranded Calling lets eligible business identity information travel in the native voice path so supported carriers can verify and display it directly on supported mobile devices. In plain English: when a verified branded call reaches a supported device, the recipient can see who is calling with more context than a phone number alone.\nFor teams that want the technical detail: Branded Calling uses Rich Call Data in the STIR/SHAKEN signing flow so supported carrier paths can verify and render branded caller identity without requiring a third-party app..\nWhat's new in the GA release\nThis GA launch is about more than what appears on the call screen. It also gives teams a better operational experience when rolling out Branded Calling.\nWith GA, customers get:\n- A self-service Console experience for onboarding and setup\n- An onboarding API to automate number registration at scale\n- An improved logo upload process with easier asset handling\n- Trust Hub consolidation for more centralized business profile and trust workflows\n- Better operational control and supportability for teams managing Branded Calling in production\nIn short: the recipient gets a more recognizable call experience, and the business gets a simpler path to launch and manage it.\nTechnical and data specifications to know\nBranded Calling still depends on carrier and device requirements, so there are a few implementation details teams should know:\n- Display Name is capped at 35 characters\n- Call Reason is capped at 64 characters\n- Both fields support ASCII characters only\n- Logos must be uploaded as 32-bit BMP files at 256x256\n- Logo validation includes a base64-encoded SHA256 digest\n- Console supports logo crop and resize during onboarding\n- Self-hosted logo URL support is available\n- Call reason is not currently supported on iOS devices due to an Apple limitation\nAvailability and rendering depend on supported carriers, devices, and configuration.\nWhy it matters: Recognition can improve the chances a call gets answered\nRecognition does more than improve appearance on the call screen. It can help legitimate outreach break through when timing matters most.\nHiya’s findings help explain why:\n- 86% do not answer unknown calls\n- 73% want businesses to identify themselves in caller ID\n- 29% say verified business name is the top factor that would make them more likely to answer\nHealthcare provider CareSignal saw a 21% increase in call answer rates after implementing Branded Calling and achieved a 48% pick-up rate. Twilio Branded Calling can reach more than 265 million mobile devices in the US. And Forrester research found that branded calling helped calls get answered more frequently and reduced the number of calls mistaken for spam.\nThat does not guarantee every call gets answered. But it can give businesses a better chance to reach customers in the moments that matter.\nThe future of outreach is programmatic trust\nWith Twilio Trust Hub, you centralize your brand identity and shift from fragmented, unverified channels to a unified, trusted communications layer. Your brand appears authentically and securely across voice, messaging, and email.\nWhile this launch focuses on voice, the need for recognizable and consistent outreach spans every channel. Twilio’s Branded Communications vision is to help businesses establish trust everywhere without managing separate identity and onboarding processes for each channel.\nBranded Calling is the starting point. It lets you present a verified identity for outbound calls today and lays the foundation for extending trust to messaging and email in the future. Enterprise trust is no longer optional, it is essential for customer engagement.\nBranded Calling is now generally available in the US. Make every call count. Explore the Branded Calling Blueprint, get started in the Twilio Console or visit our Branded Calling Docs to brand your first number.\nRelated Posts\nRelated Resources\nTwilio Docs\nFrom APIs to SDKs to sample apps\nAPI reference documentation, SDKs, helper libraries, quickstarts, and tutorials for your language and platform.\nResource Center\nThe latest ebooks, industry reports, and webinars\nLearn from customer engagement experts to improve your own communication.\nAhoy\nTwilio's developer community hub\nBest practices, code samples, and inspiration to build communications and digital engagement experiences.",
        "content": "The architecture of enterprise trust: Twilio US Branded Calling is now generally available\nTime to read:\n- Branded Calling helps businesses stand out by displaying verified business names and logos on calls.\n- This feature can improve customer trust, leading to higher answer rates and successful outreach.\n- Twilio simplifies onboarding and management, integrating verification within existing platf...",
        "tags": [
            "General"
        ],
        "location": "Global",
        "human_time": "5m ago"
    },
    {
        "title": "Guide to Migrating Your Email Sending Domain",
        "link": "https://www.twilio.com/en-us/blog/insights/how-to-migrate-email-sending-domain",
        "source": "Twilio Blog",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-13T20:07:01.362412+00:00",
        "image": "https://www.twilio.com/content/dam/twilio-com/core-assets/social/twilio-blog-default-ogimage.png",
        "full_content": "Guide to Migrating Your Email Sending Domain\nTime to read:\n- Lay technical groundwork such as SPF, DKIM, and DMARC to authenticate your new domain.\n- Gradually build sender reputation; start with low volumes, increase slowly, and maintain list hygiene.\n- Inform customers of changes; explicitly ask them to add your new domain to their safe sender list.\nThis summary was generated by AI and reviewed by the Twilio team.\nGuide to Migrating Your Email Sending Domain\nChanging your company’s name or primary domain is a massive milestone. It represents growth, evolution, and fresh branding. But behind the scenes, this shift introduces a heavy risk to your hard-earned email deliverability.\nThink of migrating to a new email domain like moving your business to a physical address in a strict, gated neighborhood. If you throw a massive block party on day one, the neighborhood watch will treat you like a public nuisance. In the email world, mailbox providers like Google and Microsoft act as that neighborhood watch. You have to introduce yourself slowly, unpack your boxes properly, and build local trust over time.\nBecause brand-new domains have zero sending history, mailbox providers treat them with extreme suspicion. Sending high volumes of email immediately from a fresh domain leads to severe deliverability issues, resulting in frustrating delays, spam folder placement, or outright blocks.\nFortunately, navigating this transition safely is not a matter of luck. Senders need a controlled, multi-phase migration strategy, focusing on technical authentication, reputation management, user awareness, and monitoring to ensure a smooth move. You do not need advanced technical skills to pull this off seamlessly.\nPhase 1: Pre-Migration Setup\nBefore you send a single email, you must lay the technical groundwork to prove your new domain's legitimacy to the neighborhood watch. Skip this phase, and your emails will be turned away at the gate.\nRespect the 30-Day Aging Mandate\nSpammers love to use newly registered, \"throwaway\" domains for malicious campaigns. Because of this, industry best practices dictate letting a newly registered domain age for at least 30 days before using it for automated or high-volume commercial sending. Think of it as letting the paint dry on your new building before inviting clients inside.\nLock Down Technical Authentication\nCertain essential DNS records must be provisioned to demonstrate compliance with mailbox provider mandates. Without these, your emails are highly likely to be rejected immediately.\n- SPF (Sender Policy Framework): Lists the specific IP addresses and servers authorized to send on your domain's behalf.\n- DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail): Adds a cryptographic signature ensuring the message hasn't been tampered with mid-transit.\n- DMARC (Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance): Instructs receiving servers exactly how to handle emails that fail SPF or DKIM checks.\nTip: Twilio SendGrid makes this easy with Automated Security, where your SPF and DKIM records are automatically managed behind the scenes. This saves you from having to manually update these DNS records every time your infrastructure changes.\nDomain Segmentation: Isolating Your Traffic\nTo protect your primary corporate domain's reputation, consider isolating all outbound automated traffic to a dedicated subdomain (e.g., info.example.com). This keeps your critical transactional emails and marketing campaigns from interfering with your team's day-to-day business communication.\nPhase 2: The Parallel Configuration & Warm-up Phase\nInstead of an immediate \"hard cut\" to the new domain, employ a parallel configuration. This approach allows traffic sent from the legacy domain to be slowly transitioned to the new domain over time.\nBy operating both infrastructures concurrently, you can safely build a positive reputation on the new domain. The more time you can allocate to this phase, the better. Rushing the process could prove incredibly challenging if you hit unexpected reputation speed bumps.\nNavigating Reverse DNS (rDNS) and Link Branding\nMost senders prefer to maintain their existing sending IP addresses, especially when they’ve already established a strong foundational reputation. However, because multiple reverse DNS records cannot exist for the same IP simultaneously, rDNS requires a hard cut at some point during the migration.\nDuring the parallel phase, it is perfectly acceptable to leave the legacy domain configured for rDNS, as it isn't the primary driver of your sender reputation. For step-by-step instructions on executing this change, check out Twilio SendGrid's official documentation on how to set up reverse DNS.\nWhile there can be only one default configuration active at a time, Link Branding will be applied automatically to match the sending domain, allowing you to easily test the new configuration. For complete instructions on making these configuration updates, review our guide on how to set up link branding.\nThe Punctuated Warm-Up: Slow and Steady Wins the Inbox\nGradually build your reputation with MBPs by slowly increasing your volume over a 2 to 8 week window.\n- Start small: Send just 100 emails a day to your most highly engaged contacts during the first few days.\n- Scale up slowly: Be careful never to double your volume too quickly from one day to the next.\n- Tailor by provider: Prioritize top inbox providers for a slower, gradual warm-up based on your specific audience makeup.\nRuthless List Hygiene: Keeping Bounces Under 2%\nHigh bounce rates on a new domain signal to MBPs that you are using a low-quality or unverified list, triggering immediate spam filtering. With a new domain, an early hit to your reputation can be incredibly difficult to overcome.\nKeep your bounce rate strictly under 2%. If you are utilizing Twilio SendGrid, the Email Validation tool can help to maintain a clean subscriber list. Additionally, you can closely monitor your bounce activity and investigate specific delivery failures using Email Logs or the Event Webhook.\nPhase 3: The Customer Awareness Campaign\nSender reputation is fueled entirely by recipient engagement. Because of this, your existing audience needs to know who you are and be explicitly informed of any upcoming changes to your brand.\nThe Pre-Launch Heads-Up\nBefore transitioning your primary volume, send an awareness campaign from your old, trusted domain to your existing customer base. This ensures the message actually reaches their inbox and primes them for the upcoming switch.\nCrafting Actionable Messaging to Spark Engagement\nYour awareness campaign should be clear, concise, and highly actionable.\n- Give explicit instructions: Ask users to add your new domain to their Safe Senders or contact list.\n- Map the brand change: Clearly explain that updates@legacybrand.com will soon become updates@newbrand.com.\n- Include an action link: Give them a reason to click a link inside the email, which provides the positive engagement signals MBPs look for.\nPhase 4: The \"Hard Cut\" & Post-Migration Vigilance\nOnce the warm-up is complete and your awareness campaign has launched, it’s time to finalize the transition.\nFlipping the Final Technical Switches\nExecute the hard cuts by updating your reverse DNS records to point definitively to the new domain and switching your default link branding over.\nMaintaining Brand Recognition in the \"Friendly From\"\nTo prevent users from deleting your emails out of confusion, include \"formerly [Legacy Brand]\" in your \"friendly from\" name for the first 3 to 6 months after deprecating the old domain.\nMonitoring Delivery Metrics Vigorously\nCarefully watch your bounces, blocks, spam reports, and deferrals. While brief deferrals are common when a domain is brand new, you should investigate further if they persist for more than two weeks.\nFrequently Asked Questions\nHow long does a domain migration typically take?\nA proper domain migration usually takes between 2 to 8 weeks. The exact timeline depends heavily on your overall sending volume and the responsiveness of your audience during the warm-up phase.\nCan I use the exact same IP address for my new domain?\nYes. In fact, maintaining the same sending IP addresses is recommended if you have already established a strong foundational reputation. Just remember that your reverse DNS (rDNS) will require a \"hard cut\" to the new domain at some point during the transition.\nWhat happens if my emails start going to spam during the warm-up?\nIf you notice a sudden drop in engagement or an increase in blocks, pause your volume increases immediately. Keep your sending volume steady or scale it back slightly while focusing exclusively on your most engaged users until deliverability stabilizes.\nNext Steps\nA domain migration is a major corporate milestone. With a controlled, multi-phase approach, you can transition seamlessly without sacrificing your deliverability. Adhering to these technical authentication and warm-up best practices helps you establish a highly trusted sender reputation on your new domain.\nAs you navigate the warm-up process, remember that patience and a commitment to best practices will lead to improved email deliverability and stronger customer relationships. Ready to plan your migration with total confidence? Contact our Twilio Expert Services team today for a guided warm-up experience tailored specifically to your business.\nRelated Posts\nRelated Resources\nTwilio Docs\nFrom APIs to SDKs to sample apps\nAPI reference documentation, SDKs, helper libraries, quickstarts, and tutorials for your language and platform.\nResource Center\nThe latest ebooks, industry reports, and webinars\nLearn from customer engagement experts to improve your own communication.\nAhoy\nTwilio's developer community hub\nBest practices, code samples, and inspiration to build communications and digital engagement experiences.",
